{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2720 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "487ca040cf6dcdcd56e02ecd4049779d42730ecadad4a06a06f8d0aed6a7882f" :edb/db-before-t 2719 :edb/db-after-t 2720 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536032 50 #inst "2026-10-15T20:47:45.883Z" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186053211 1078 #edb/ref 17592186053215 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186053223 1078 #edb/ref 17592186053226 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054451 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n\n- Subtract $x$ from $10.$\n\n- $y^2 -2  \\leq 3$\n\n- $1+z$ is a perfect square." 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186054451 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n\nI. Subtract $x$ from $10.$\n\nII. $y^2 -2  \\leq 3$\n\nIII. $1+z$ is a perfect square." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054551 1071 "In a class, each student selected one course among the two possible options, math or physics. The results are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Math | Physics | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $12$ | $12$ | $24$ |\n| Male | $20$ | $6$ | $26$ |\n| Totals | $32$ | $18$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student selected physics or is male (or both)." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054551 1071 "In a class, each student selected one course among the two possible options, math or physics. The results are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Math | Physics | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Female | $12$ | $12$ | $24$ |\n| Male | $20$ | $6$ | $26$ |\n| Totals | $32$ | $18$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student selected physics or is male (or both)." 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186054551 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student selected physics or is male, we\n\n- add up the numbers that are in the column **\"Physics\"** or the row **\"Male\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the column **\"Physics\"** or the row **\"Male\"** are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Math | Physics | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $12$ | $\\color{blue}12$ | $24$ |\n| Male | $\\color{blue}20$ | $\\color{blue}6$ | $26$ |\n| Totals | $32$ | $18$ | $50$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(50),$ we get\n\n$$\np =\\dfrac{12+20+6}{50} = \\dfrac{38}{50} =0.76.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054551 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student selected physics or is male, we\n\n- add up the numbers that are in the column **\"Physics\"** or the row **\"Male\"**, and then\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the column **\"Physics\"** or the row **\"Male\"** are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Math | Physics | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Female | $12$ | $\\color{blue}12$ | $24$ |\n| Male | $\\color{blue}20$ | $\\color{blue}6$ | $26$ |\n| Totals | $32$ | $18$ | $50$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(50)$, we get\n\n$$\np =\\dfrac{12+20+6}{50} = \\dfrac{38}{50} =0.76\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186054552 1071 "Anna surveys some students in a school, asking them what pets out of cats or dogs they have at home. Her findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Has a cat | $30$ | $56$ | $86$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $60$ | $54$ | $114$ |\n| Totals | $90$ | $110$ | $200$ |\n\nLet $C$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the school **\"Has a cat\"**, and $D$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the school **\"Has a dog\"**. Estimate $P(C \\cap D)$." 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186054552 1071 "Anna surveys some students in a school, asking them what pets out of cats or dogs they have at home. Her findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below. \n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $30$ | $56$ | $86$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $60$ | $54$ | $114$ |\n| Totals | $90$ | $110$ | $200$ |\n\nLet $C$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the school **\"Has a cat\"**, and $D$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the school **\"Has a dog\"**. Estimate $P(C \\cap D).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054552 1074 "The event $C \\cap D$ is the event that a randomly selected student in the school has both a cat and a dog.\n\nTo estimate the probability of $C \\cap D$, we need to\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a cat\"** and the column **\"Has a dog\"**, and then\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a cat\"** and the column **\"Has a dog\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Has a cat | $\\color{blue}30$ | $56$ | $86$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $60$ | $54$ | $114$ |\n| Totals | $90$ | $110$ | $200$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(200)$, we get\n\n$$\nP(C \\cap D) = \\dfrac{30}{200} = 0.15\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186054552 1074 "The event $C \\cap D$ is the event that a randomly selected student in the school has both a cat and a dog.\n\nTo estimate the probability of $C \\cap D,$ we need to \n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a cat\"** and the column **\"Has a dog\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a cat\"** and the column **\"Has a dog\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $\\color{blue}30$ | $56$ | $86$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $60$ | $54$ | $114$ |\n| Totals | $90$ | $110$ | $200$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(200),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(C \\cap D) = \\dfrac{30}{200} = 0.15.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054553 1071 "There are $250$ boys and $250$ girls in a school. Of them, $40$ boys and $40$ girls were randomly selected and asked which sport they prefer between volleyball, basketball, football, or soccer. The results of the survey are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Volleyball | Basketball | Football | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girl | $14$ | $10$ | $2$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Boy | $8$ | $12$ | $11$ | $9$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $22$ | $22$ | $13$ | $23$ | $80$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly selected student in the survey is a boy who prefers football or soccer, and then use this to estimate the number of students in the entire school who are boys that prefer football or soccer." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054553 1071 "There are $250$ boys and $250$ girls in a school. Of them, $40$ boys and $40$ girls were randomly selected and asked which sport they prefer between volleyball, basketball, football, or soccer. The results of the survey are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Volleyball | Basketball | Football | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Girl | $14$ | $10$ | $2$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Boy | $8$ | $12$ | $11$ | $9$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $22$ | $22$ | $13$ | $23$ | $80$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly selected student in the survey is a boy who prefers football or soccer, and then use this to estimate the number of students in the entire school who are boys that prefer football or soccer." 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186054553 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected student is a boy who prefers football or soccer, we\n\n- add up the numbers at the intersection of the row **\"Boy\"** and the columns **\"Football\"** and **\"Soccer\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers at the intersection of the row **\"Boy\"** and the columns **\"Football\"** and **\"Soccer\"** are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Volleyball | Basketball | Football | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girl | $14$ | $10$ | $2$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Boy | $8$ | $12$ | $\\color{blue}11$ | $\\color{blue}9$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $22$ | $22$ | $13$ | $23$ | $80$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(80),$ we get\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{11+9}{80} = \\dfrac{20}{80} = 0.25.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student is a boy who prefers football or soccer.\n\nFinally, to estimate the number of students in the entire school who are boys that prefer football or soccer, we multiply the total number of students in the school $(500)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =500\\cdot p \\\\  & =500\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =125\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054553 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected student is a boy who prefers football or soccer, we\n\n- add up the numbers at the intersection of the row **\"Boy\"** and the columns **\"Football\"** and **\"Soccer\"**, and then\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers at the intersection of the row **\"Boy\"** and the columns **\"Football\"** and **\"Soccer\"** are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Volleyball | Basketball | Football | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Girl | $14$ | $10$ | $2$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Boy | $8$ | $12$ | $\\color{blue}11$ | $\\color{blue}9$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $22$ | $22$ | $13$ | $23$ | $80$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(80)$, we get\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{11+9}{80} = \\dfrac{20}{80} = 0.25\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student is a boy who prefers football or soccer.\n\nFinally, to estimate the number of students in the entire school who are boys that prefer football or soccer, we multiply the total number of students in the school $(500)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 500 \\cdot p \\\\\n&= 500 \\cdot 0.25 \\\\\n&= 125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186054661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559810 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559833 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559873 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559875 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559817 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559867 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559779 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559841 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559865 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559866 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559869 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559776 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559843 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559852 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559775 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559805 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559799 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559884 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054881 1050 480.0 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186054913 1050 420.0 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186055329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186055329 1071 "A group of randomly selected students from School A was asked about whether or not they use TV or the internet to access the local news. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | TV | No TV | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Internet | $90$ | $15$ | $105$ |\n| No internet | $30$ | $15$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $120$ | $30$ | $150$ |\n\nThere are $100$ students in School B. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same for both schools, \nestimate the number of School B students that access the local news either from TV or the internet, but not both." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186055329 1071 "A group of randomly selected students from School A was asked about whether or not they use TV or the internet to access the local news. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | TV | No TV | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Internet | $90$ | $15$ | $105$ |\n| No internet | $30$ | $15$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $120$ | $30$ | $150$ |\n\nThere are $100$ students in School B. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same for both schools,\nestimate the number of School B students that access the local news either from TV or the internet, but not both.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186055329 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected student gets the local news either from TV or the internet but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"No internet\"** or the column **\"TV\"**, but not in both, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | TV | No TV | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Internet | $90$ | $15$ | $105$ |\n| No internet | $30$ | $15$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $120$ | $30$ | $150$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(150),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{30+15}{150}=\\frac{45}{150}=0.3.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student gets the local news from TV or the internet but not both.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of students of School B that get their local news either from TV or the internet but not both, we multiply the total number of students in the school $(100)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =100\\cdot p \\\\  & =100\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186055331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186055331 1071 "In a high school, a group of students was asked about whether they prefer apples, oranges, or bananas. The results are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Apple | Orange | Banana | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $11$ | $8$ | $7$ | $26$ |\n| Boys | $12$ | $6$ | $6$ | $24$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $14$ | $13$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the group preferred apples or bananas or is a girl." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186055331 1071 "In a high school, a group of students was asked about whether they prefer apples, oranges, or bananas. The results are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Apple | Orange | Banana | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Girls | $11$ | $8$ | $7$ | $26$ |\n| Boys | $12$ | $6$ | $6$ | $24$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $14$ | $13$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the group preferred apples or bananas or is a girl.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186055331 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student preferred apples or bananas or is a girl, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the columns **\"Apple\"** or **\"Banana\"** or the row **\"Girls\"**, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the columns **\"Apple\"** or **\"Banana\"** or the row **\"Girls\"** are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Apple | Orange | Banana | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $11$ | $8$ | $7$ | $26$ |\n| Boys | $12$ | $6$ | $6$ | $24$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $14$ | $13$ | $50$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(50),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{11+12+8+7+6}{50}=\\frac{44}{50}=0.88.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186055977 1064 "Sometimes, we want to find the probability that two or more events co-occur (occur together). Events like this, involving a combination of two or more events, are called **compound events**.\n\nFor instance, the $100$ members of a volleyball club were asked about their preferences between Chinese food and Italian pizza. The results of the survey are represented in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Male | $20$ | $35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $20$ | $25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nSuppose that we want to find the probability that a randomly selected club member is a male that prefers pizza. \n\nFirst, let $A$ be the event that a randomly selected member is **\"Male\"** and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected member prefers **\"Italian pizza\"**. \n\nThe probability that $A$ *and* $B$ co-occur, denoted by $P(A\\cap B)$ and called the **intersection**, is found by identifying the intersection of these events in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the column **\"Italian pizza\"** and the row **\"Male\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Male | $20$ | $\\color{blue}35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $20$ | $25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nDividing this number by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{35}{100} \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the experimental probability that a randomly selected member prefers Italian pizza and is male." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186055977 1064 "Sometimes, we want to find the probability that two or more events co-occur (occur together). Events like this, involving a combination of two or more events, are called **compound events**.\n\nFor instance, the $100$ members of a volleyball club were asked about their preferences between Chinese food and Italian pizza. The results of the survey are represented in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $20$ | $35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $20$ | $25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nSuppose that we want to find the probability that a randomly selected club member is a male that prefers pizza.\n\nFirst, let $A$ be the event a randomly selected member is **\"Male\"** and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected member prefers **\"Italian pizza\"**.\n\nThe probability that $A$ *and* $B$ co-occur, denoted by $P(A\\cap B)$ and called the **intersection**, is found by identifying the intersection of these events in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the column **\"Italian pizza\"** and the row **\"Male\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $20$ | $\\color{blue}35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $20$ | $25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nDividing this number by the total number of data points in the table $(100)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∩B) &= \\frac{35}{100} \\\\\n&= 0.35\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the experimental probability that a randomly selected member prefers Italian pizza and is male." 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186055990 1064 "Let's return to our first example, where the Volleyball club members were asked about whether they preferred Italian pizza or Chinese food. The data is shown below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Male | $20$ | $35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $20$ | $25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nSuppose we want to find the probability that a randomly selected club member either prefers Chinese food *or* is female (or both).\n\nLet $A$ be the event that a randomly selected member prefers **\"Chinese food\"** and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected member is **\"Female\"**.\n\nThe probability that $A$ *or* $B$ (or both) occurs, denoted by $P(A\\cup B)$ and called the **union**, is found by counting the number of data points in which $A$ or $B$ occurred and dividing by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers that are in *either* the column **\"Chinese food\"** *or* the row **\"Female\"** (or both) are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Male | $\\color{blue}20$ | $35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $\\color{blue}20$ | $\\color{blue}25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =\\frac{20+20+25}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{65}{100} \\\\  & =0.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the experimental probability that a randomly selected member prefers Chinese food or is female." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186055990 1064 "Let's return to our first example, where the Volleyball club members were asked about whether they preferred Italian pizza or Chinese food. The data is shown below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $20$ | $35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $20$ | $25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nSuppose we want to find the probability that a randomly selected club member either prefers Chinese food *or* is female (or both).\n\nLet $A$ be the event that a randomly selected member prefers **\"Chinese food\"** and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected member is **\"Female\"**.\n\nThe probability that $A$ *or* $B$ (or both) occurs, denoted by $P(A\\cup B)$ and called the **union**, is found by counting the number of data points in which $A$ or $B$ occurred and dividing by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers that are in *either* the column **\"Chinese food\"** *or* the row **\"Female\"** (or both) are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $\\color{blue}20$ | $35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $\\color{blue}20$ | $\\color{blue}25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪B) &= \\frac{20 + 20 + 25}{100} \\\\\n&= \\frac{65}{100} \\\\\n&= 0.65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the experimental probability that a randomly selected member prefers Chinese food or is female." 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186056004 1064 "A **mathematical statement** is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either **true** or **false**.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The sentence, \"The sum of $1$ and $2$ is $3$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *true.*\n\n- Likewise, \"$2+2=5$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *false.*\n\nThe following examples are *not* mathematical statements:\n\n- \"Find the sum of $1$ and $2$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is an instruction.\n\n- \"What is the sum of $1$ and $2?$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is a question.\n\n- Similarly, \"$6+9$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\nWe often use upper-case letters such as $P, Q,$ and $R$ to represent statements. For example:\n\n- $P:$ The sum of two even integers is odd.\n\n- $Q: 3 \\in \\mathbb Z$\n\n- $R: \\{2,4,6\\ldots\\}\\cup \\{1,3,5,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$\n\nIn this case, the statements $Q$ and $R$ are true, whereas statement $P$ is false." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186056004 1064 "A **mathematical statement** is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either **true** or **false**.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The sentence, \"The sum of $1$ and $2$ is $3$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *true.*\n\n- Likewise, \"$2+2=5$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *false.*\n\nThe following examples are *not* mathematical statements:\n\n- \"Find the sum of $1$ and $2$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is an instruction.\n\n- \"What is the sum of $1$ and $2?$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is a question.\n\n- Similarly, \"$6+9$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\nWe often use upper-case letters such as $P, Q,$ and $R$ to represent statements. For example:\n\n- $P:$ The sum of two even integers is odd.\n\n- $Q: 3 \\in \\mathbb Z$\n\n- $R: \\{2,4,6\\ldots\\}\\cup \\{1,3,5,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$\n\nIn this case, the statements $Q$ and $R$ are true, whereas statement $P$ is false.\n\nWe often refer to the **truth value** of a mathematical statement. We use [math]T[/math] to denote \"true\" and [math]F[/math] to denote \"false.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186056051 1064 "A **predicate** (or **open sentence**) of one variable is a sentence that contains a variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute a concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a mathematical statement that is either *true* or *false*.\n\nFor example, consider the following predicate:\n\n$x > 1$\n\nThis is a predicate because substituting concrete values for $x$ gives a mathematical statement.\n\nFor instance: \n\n- Substituting $x=2$ gives the *true* statement \"$2 > 1$\"\n\n- Substituting $x=5$ gives the *true* statement \"$5 > 1$\"\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ gives the *false* statement \"$0 > 1$\"\n\nWe denote predicates using capital letters and the corresponding variable in parentheses. In this example, we can write our predicate more formally as follows:\n\n$P(x):$ $x > 1$\n\nAny predicate must be defined with its **universal set,** which is the set from which we are allowed to pull the concrete values of the variable. So, we could state our predicate more formally as\n\n$P(x):$ $x > 1,$ $\\quad$ where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nThe universal set can often be identified from the context and doesn't need to be explicitly stated. For example, if we have the predicate \n\n$n$ is divisible by 5,\n\nwe can assume that $n$ is defined on the set of all integers, i.e., $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$\n\nIn math, *any equation or inequality is a predicate*. However, a sentence containing a variable is not necessarily a predicate. For example, the sentence \n\n$\\qquad$ \"What is the sign of $y?$\"\n\nis *not* a predicate because substituting a concrete value for $y$ does not give a mathematical statement. Instead, it gives a question. \n\nSimilarly, \n\n$z^2-5$\n\nis *not* a predicate either. It's just an expression." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186056051 1064 "A **predicate** (or **open sentence**) of one variable is a sentence that contains a variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute a concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes 