edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186074180 1071 "The point $P(5, 6)$ is rotated $90^{∘}$ clockwise about the origin $O$ and then translated according to the function $(x, y) ↦ (x + k, y + k)$. If the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations is $(12, 1)$, then $k =$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186074180 1071 "The point $P(5,6)$ is rotated ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise about the origin $O$ and then translated according to the function $(x,y)↦(x+k,y+k).$ If the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations is $(12,1),$ then $k=$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186074180 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA rotation of ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise about $O$ can be represented by\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(y,-x).\n$$\n\nApplying this to the point $P,$ we get\n\n$$\n(5,6)↦(6,-5).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, applying the translation to $(6,-5),$ we get\n\n$$\n(6,-5)↦(6+k,\\,-5+k).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the resulting point is $(12,1),$ by equating the $x$- and $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}6+k=12 \\\\ -5+k=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,k & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=6.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186075730 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559558 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186075730 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559558 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186075732 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559345 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186075732 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559345 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186078559 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559848 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186078797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186078797 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of a sequence if the $4$th term is $-135$ and the common ratio is $-3$.$a_{n} =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186078797 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of a sequence if the $4$th term is $-135$ and the common ratio is $-3.$\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186078797 1074 "The common ratio is $r=-3,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $4$th term is ${a}_{4}=-135,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{4-1} \\\\ -135 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{3} \\\\ -135 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-27) \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=5,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5(-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186078799 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186078799 1071 "If the fifth term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{8}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{4}$, then the eighth term of this sequence is {{field-1}}." 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186078799 1071 "If the fifth term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{8}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{4},$ then the eighth term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186078799 1074 "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{4},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{8},$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{5-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{256}) \\\\ \\frac{256}{8} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 32 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=32,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the $8$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{8-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{7} \\\\  & ={2}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{({2}^{2})^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{2}^{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is ${a}_{8}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186081119 1078 #edb/ref 17592186081122 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186081629 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081630 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186081629 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081631 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186081629 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081632 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186081629 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081633 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186081629 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081634 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186081629 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559033 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186081629 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559102 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186081629 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559393 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186081629 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559399 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186081629 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559432 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186081629 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559033 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186082919 1074 "Let us call $A$ the event \"The patient is a smoker\" and $B$ the event \"The patient has diabetes.\"\n\nSince none of the diabetic patients are smokers, we draw two non-overlapping circles to represent the events $A$ and $B$.\n\n- Since there are $10$ smokers, we write the