se] [17592186120373 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ What is $(2u)\\cdot v?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186120373 1074 "First, multiplying each component of $u$ by $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2u)\\cdot v & =2\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4+10+2+0 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186121033 1064 "In general, if we have two vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ and a scalar $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R},$ then our vector operations are defined as follows:\n\n- The **sum/difference** of the two vectors is defined as\n![](images/fc/fc78b2f4e9262108a217e57fec67e096b8b8b18e1486bec8e51df60b8afdfdfa.svg)\n\n- **Scalar multiplication** of a vector is defined as\n![](images/e7/e78025f3481423365b2e924b6f8ba4e59f41e0c644ced7d2263238b219cba27a.svg)\n\n- The **dot product** of two vectors is defined as\n\n$$\n\\quad \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = \\underbrace{u_1 \\cdot v_1 + u_2 \\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n \\cdot v_n}_{\\Large{n} \\,\\textrm{-terms}} = \\sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186121033 1064 "In general, if we have two vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ and a scalar $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R},$ then our vector operations are defined as follows:\n\n1\\. The **sum/difference** of the two vectors is defined as\n  ![](images/fc/fc78b2f4e9262108a217e57fec67e096b8b8b18e1486bec8e51df60b8afdfdfa.svg)\n\n2\\. **Scalar multiplication** of a vector is defined as\n  ![](images/e7/e78025f3481423365b2e924b6f8ba4e59f41e0c644ced7d2263238b219cba27a.svg)\n\n3\\. The **dot product** of two vectors is defined as\n\n  $$\n  \\quad \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = \\underbrace{u_1 \\cdot v_1 + u_2 \\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n \\cdot v_n}_{\\Large{n} \\,\\textrm{-terms}} = \\sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i.\n  $$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186121062 1064 "To add two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we just need to add their corresponding components. To illustrate, consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4,$ given by\n\n![](images/1f/1f1b79f2e61fccbb27944284cbdd217a311a7f795100cfbb56340a457814b407.svg)\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b},$ all we have to do is to add each component of the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ with the corresponding component of $\\mathbf{b}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5+(-5) \\\\ 1+3 \\\\ -3+1 \\\\ 6+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can only compute the sum, difference, or dot product of two vectors if both vectors have the same number of components. For example, we can't find the following sum, so we say it's *undefined*:\n![](images/6c/6c377966aa9fa1d581c0f9e3ba16bceb4020471914d5c4fb61118ff7cd709466.svg)" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186121062 1064 "To add two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we just need to add their corresponding components. To illustrate, consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4,$ given by\n\n![](images/1f/1f1b79f2e61fccbb27944284cbdd217a311a7f795100cfbb56340a457814b407.svg)\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b},$ all we have to do is to add each component of the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ with the corresponding component of $\\mathbf{b}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5+(-5) \\\\ 1+3 \\\\ -3+1 \\\\ 6+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can only compute the sum, difference, or dot product of two vectors if both vectors have the same number of components. For example, we can't find the following sum, so we say it's *undefined*:\n![](images/6c/6c377966aa9fa1d581c0f9e3ba16bceb4020471914d5c4fb61118ff7cd709466.svg)" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186124465 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559814 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186124465 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559831 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186124466 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559782 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186124466 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559816 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186124557 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559825 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186129309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559786 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186133850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559890 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186133906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559796 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186138458 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559784 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186138526 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559802 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186145256 1078 #edb/ref 17592186145257 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186146924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146925 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186146924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146927 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186146924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146928 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186146924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559107 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186146924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559254 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186146924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559282 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186146924 1078 #edb/ref 17592186146926 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186147343 1071 "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- The planes intersect on a straight line." 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186147343 1071 "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nII. The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nIII. The planes intersect on a straight line." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186147425 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559772 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186147425 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559889 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186147427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559877 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186147645 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559804 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186147646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559809 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186147646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559842 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186147647 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559829 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186147917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186147917 1074 "The rectangular prism has $6$ rectangular faces, so it has $6$ faces in total." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186148195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186148195 1071 "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-5=0 \\\\ 2x-y+z+1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186148195 1071 "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n${x + 2y - 5 = 0; 2x - y + z + 1 = 0$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186148195 1074 "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,0⟩\\times ⟨2,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2i-j-5k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186150503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150504 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186150503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150505 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186150503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150506 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186150503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150507 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186150503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150508 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186150503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559055 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186150503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559116 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186150503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559192 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186150503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559291 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186150503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559332 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186150503 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559332 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186152052 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559838 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186152054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559820 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186152271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559857 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186152272 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559811 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186152272 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559849 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186152666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186152666 1071 "Jordan wants to paint the outside of his home. Using $2$ gallons of paint, he was able to paint $450$ square feet of the surface. There is a proportional relationship between the volume of paint $x$ (in gallons) that Jordan uses and the area $y$ (in square feet) he paints. What is the equation that represents the relationship between $x$ and $y$?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186152666 1071 "Jordan wants to paint the outside of his home. Using $2$ gallons of paint, he was able to paint $450$ square feet of the surface. There is a proportional relationship between the volume of paint $x$ (in gallons) that Jordan uses and the area $y$ (in square feet) he paints. What is the equation that represents the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186152666 1074 "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that Jordan painted $y=450$ square feet using $x=2$ gallons of paint.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{450}{2}=225\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=225x.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186162013 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559785 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186162013 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559800 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186162097 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559792 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186165051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165052 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186165051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165053 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186165051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165054 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186165051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165055 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186165051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165056 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186165051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559195 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186165051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559325 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186165051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559384 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186165051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559442 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186165051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559562 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186165051 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559195 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186166751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559815 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186166751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559880 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186166752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559788 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186166752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559870 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186167307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186167307 1071 "$$\n{A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Find the matrix $C$ given that $B=\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\},$ and the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $B$ are $[C]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186167307 1071 "$A_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}, A_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Find the matrix $C$ given that $B = {A_{1}, A_{2}}$ is a basis of $Span{A_{1}, A_{2}}$, and the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $B$ are $[C]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186167307 1074 "Any vector in $\\text{Span}\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{A}_{1}+{x}_{2}{A}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n[C]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]+(-3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -15 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -11 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186168064 1064 "We've already seen how to write the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf v\\in \\mathbb R^n$ relative some general basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\mathbb R^n.$ In this lesson, we will generalize this concept to abstract vector spaces.\n\nAs an example, consider the matrices $A_1$ and $A_2,$ given by\n\n![](images/28/2828c037a750f078b4d98b93248e800ec05d4899d7eb390dd135326314c44e70.svg)\n\nThe set $\\mathcal{B}=\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of the vector space $V = \\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}.$ Now, suppose that the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are \n\n![](images/2d/2db3ab1e4452eff2f19151cc87a805452433c7f305a80e5480c25241cba42c38.svg)\n\nLet's find the matrix $C$ using this information. \n\nAs usual, any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\nx_1 A_1  + x_2 A_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1,x_2$ are the coordinates of the vector relative to the basis. \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n![](images/64/6489e130a9237881664e1649cc5408cdff87cfb12dcf44c0342b9961cdf7bc81.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 12 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 10 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186168064 1064 "We've already seen how to write the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf v\\in \\mathbb R^n$ relative some general basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\mathbb R^n.$ In this lesson, we will generalize this concept to abstract vector spaces.\n\nAs an example, consider the matrices $A_1$ and $A_2,$ given by\n\n![](images/28/2828c037a750f078b4d98b93248e800ec05d4899d7eb390dd135326314c44e70.svg)\n\nThe set $\\mathcal{B}=\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of the vector space $V = \\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}.$ Now, suppose that the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are \n\n![](images/2d/2db3ab1e4452eff2f19151cc87a805452433c7f305a80e5480c25241cba42c38.svg)\n\nLet's find the matrix $C$ using this information. \n\nAs usual, any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\nx_1 A_1  + x_2 A_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1,x_2$ are the coordinates of the vector relative to the basis. \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n![](images/64/6489e130a9237881664e1649cc5408cdff87cfb12dcf44c0342b9961cdf7bc81.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 12 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 10 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186171387 1078 #edb/ref 17592186171392 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186171837 1071 "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,1]?$\n\n- $p: \\{ 0, 1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $q: \\mathbb{R} \\to [ 0, 1 ]$\n\n- $r: [0,1] \\to [ 0, 1 )$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186171837 1071 "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,1]?$\n\nI. $p: \\{ 0, 1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}$\n\nII. $q: \\mathbb{R} \\to [ 0, 1 ]$\n\nIII. $r: [0,1] \\to [ 0, 1 )$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186171838 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$.\"*\n\n- $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\n- $g: Y \\to \\mathbb{Q}$\n\n- $g:\\mathbb{R} \\to Y$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186171838 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$.\"*\n\nI. $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\nII. $g: Y \\to \\mathbb{Q}$\n\nIII. $g:\\mathbb{R} \\to Y$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186171839 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $g(x)=x-x^3?$\n\n- $g:x \\mapsto x-x^3$\n\n- $g:x \\mapsto -x^3$\n\n- $g:-x \\mapsto x^3$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186171839 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $g(x)=x-x^3?$\n\nI. $g:x \\mapsto x-x^3$\n\nII. $g:x \\mapsto -x^3$\n\nIII. $g:-x \\mapsto x^3$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186171840 1071 "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$\n\n- The codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- The range of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186171840 1071 "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}?$\n\nI. The domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$\n\nII. The codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$\n\nIII. The range of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172031 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559773 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172031 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559808 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172031 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559883 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172032 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559790 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172032 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559828 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172032 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559876 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172032 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559888 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172033 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559794 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172033 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559832 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172034 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559780 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172034 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559844 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172111 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559824 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172165 1050 420.0 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172627 1071 "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0, 3)$?\n\n1. $p: {0, 3} → R$\n2. $q: R → [0, 3)$\n3. $r: [0, 3) → {0, 3}$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186172627 1071 "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,3)?$\n\nI. $p:\\{0,3\\}\\to R$\n\nII. $q:R\\to [0,3)$\n\nIII. $r:[0,3)\\to \\{0,3\\}$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186172627 1074 "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[0,3),$ the interval from $0$ to $3$ (including $0$ but not including $3$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $\\{0,3\\},$ the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $3$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186175540 1078 #edb/ref 17592186175544 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186175908 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559812 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186175908 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559882 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186175908 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559887 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186175946 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559872 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186176014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559774 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186176014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559783 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186176014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559886 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186176078 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559835 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186176078 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559859 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186176252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173770 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186176567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186176567 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(3-\\lambda )^{4}(5+\\lambda )^{6},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{12}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186176567 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(λ) = λ^{2}(3 - λ)^{4}(5 + λ)^{6}$, and for the eigenvalue $λ =-5$, there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of $R^{12}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $λ =-5$?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186176567 1074 "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(3-\\lambda )^{4}(5+\\lambda )^{6},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-5$ has multiplicity $6.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ is $6.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-5$ is $6-2=4.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186176994 1064 "For some $n\\times n$ matrices, it's possible to construct a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting entirely of linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = 5, \\lambda_2 = 4,$ and $\\lambda_3 = 2,$ with corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors \n\n![](images/45/451c66729d04278ec42f1ffcc558245b5cd2ef14b0c3d14dbd35447702b7b873.svg)\nThese eigenvectors form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nHowever, there exist $n\\times n$ matrices whose eigenvectors do *not* form a basis of $\\mathbb R^n.$ \n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n![](images/65/653166c6712102e8afacb5e2a73de617a3777e6812d9abbeb760f72b664525b3.svg)\n\nhas the single eigenvalue ${\\color{black}\\lambda=4}$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ However, there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, namely\n\n![](images/50/5007b5e49662ccb6aad508b233f4d107bdb89afc2607eee76280a16ec02d6f90.svg)\n\nSince there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we say that the **geometric multiplicity** of ${\\color{black}\\lambda=4}$ equals  $2.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186176994 1064 "For some $n\\times n$ matrices, it's possible to construct a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting entirely of linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = 5, \\lambda_2 = 4,$ and $\\lambda_3 = 2,$ with corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors \n\n![](images/45/451c66729d04278ec42f1ffcc558245b5cd2ef14b0c3d14dbd35447702b7b873.svg)\nThese eigenvectors form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nHowever, there exist $n\\times n$ matrices whose eigenvectors do *not* form a basis of $\\mathbb R^n.$ \n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n![](images/65/653166c6712102e8afacb5e2a73de617a3777e6812d9abbeb760f72b664525b3.svg)\n\nhas the single eigenvalue ${\\color{black}\\lambda=4}$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ However, there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, namely\n\n![](images/50/5007b5e49662ccb6aad508b233f4d107bdb89afc2607eee76280a16ec02d6f90.svg)\n\nSince there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we say that the **geometric multiplicity** of ${\\color{black}\\lambda=4}$ equals  $2.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186177053 1064 "So, not every $n\\times n$ matrix has the property that its eigenvectors form a basis of $\\mathbb R^n.$\n\nDespite this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*For any real $n \\times n$ matrix, there is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting of generalized eigenvectors of the matrix.*\n\nTo illustrate, let's go back to our matrix \n![](images/d0/d09805a20bbc23954fdec5a893e05ec0bad45a6503e27354df60b4d71952f990.svg)\n\nwhere the eigenvectors corresponding to the single eigenvalue $\\lambda = 4$ are\n\n![](images/e6/e6053d03e5e0f4bc843856d88dbc9e8e14252200a5d7280a4a05872b19bd4d56.svg)\n\nRemember that $\\lambda = 4$ has *algebraic* multiplicity $3$ and *geometric* multiplicity $2.$\n\nNow, it's easy to show that $A$ has a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ given by\n\n![](images/89/890d3b69aa88f0f442760d03afc7333d2e918b5b3401a33b167cfa40321a5f4c.svg)\nand our generalized eigenvectors form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, ${\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{u}}}, {\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{v}}},$ and $\\color{red}\\mathbf{w}$ form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ In particular, our basis consists of\n\n- two eigenvectors ($\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}$), and\n\n- one generalized eigenvector whose rank is greater than one ($\\color{red}\\mathbf{w}$).\n\nNotice that the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{algebraic multiplicity}-\\text{geometric multiplicity} & =3-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is no coincidence. In general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ equals the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda.$*" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186177053 1064 "So, not every $n\\times n$ matrix has the property that its eigenvectors form a basis of $\\mathbb R^n.$\n\nDespite this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*For any real $n \\times n$ matrix, there is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting of generalized eigenvectors of the matrix.*\n\nTo illustrate, let's go back to our matrix \n![](images/d0/d09805a20bbc23954fdec5a893e05ec0bad45a6503e27354df60b4d71952f990.svg)\n\nwhere the eigenvectors corresponding to the single eigenvalue $\\lambda = 4$ are\n\n![](images/e6/e6053d03e5e0f4bc843856d88dbc9e8e14252200a5d7280a4a05872b19bd4d56.svg)\n\nRemember that $\\lambda = 4$ has *algebraic* multiplicity $3$ and *geometric* multiplicity $2.$\n\nNow, it's easy to show that $A$ has a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ given by\n\n![](images/89/890d3b69aa88f0f442760d03afc7333d2e918b5b3401a33b167cfa40321a5f4c.svg)\nand our generalized eigenvectors form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, ${\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{u}}}, {\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{v}}},$ and $\\color{red}\\mathbf{w}$ form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ In particular, our basis consists of\n\n- two eigenvectors ($\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}$), and\n\n- one generalized eigenvector whose rank is greater than one ($\\color{red}\\mathbf{w}$).\n\nNotice that the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{algebraic multiplicity}-\\text{geometric multiplicity} & =3-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is no coincidence. In general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ equals the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda.$*" 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186184197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559855 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186184197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559891 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186221417 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559846 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186221418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559818 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186221419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559885 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186234062 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559659 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186338315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337292 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186338315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337566 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186338315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337575 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186338315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337875 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186338315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337921 13194139536032 17592186045417 false] [17592186338315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559016 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186338315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559174 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186338315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559289 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186338315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559318 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186338315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559581 13194139536032 175921860