ue] [17592186559769 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559145 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559769 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559267 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559769 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559568 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559769 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559595 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559769 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559769 1077 "selection" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559769 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559595 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559769 1079 #uuid "fb3d5956-200b-5c5c-9bbb-4da333869f74" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559770 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559392 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559770 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559770 1077 "field-1" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559770 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559392 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559770 1079 #uuid "fc4e3ec0-7449-5311-a9a7-e21685e5a48c" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559182 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559232 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559423 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559447 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559462 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559771 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559771 1077 "selection" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559771 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559232 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559771 1079 #uuid "fff9c6a5-d7fa-5ed1-9590-bb662eb6e249" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559741 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559772 1069 "q-71392" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559772 1071 "The intersection of the planes $-x+z-1=0$ and $y+z+5=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559772 1072 #uuid "0174198f-a0ad-5395-9af8-f36d4c14897b" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559772 1074 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n-x+0-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\ny+0+5=0\\,⟹\\,y=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨-1,-5,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+z-1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $y+z+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-1,-5,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1+(-5)=-6,$ and $b=⟨-1,1,-1⟩.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559695 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559773 1069 "q-96750" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559773 1071 "Which of the following functions has the domain $(0,2)?$\n\nI. $p:(0,2)\\to \\{0,2\\}$\n\nII. $q:R\\to (0,2)$\n\nIII. $r:(0,2)\\to R$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559773 1072 #uuid "03451249-6784-5494-bb32-85da8e4ecff7" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559773 1074 "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is $(0,2),$ the interval from $0$ to $2$ (not including $0$ nor $2$).\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $(0,2),$ the interval from $0$ to $2$ (not including $0$ nor $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559742 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559774 1069 "q-116321" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559774 1071 "The irradiance $I,$ in $W/{m}^{2},$ of a lamp placed in the center of a spherical chamber varies inversely with the **square** of the radius $r$ of the spherical chamber, measured in meters. It is known that the irradiance is $0.6\\,W/{m}^{2}$ when the radius of the chamber is $3\\,m.$ What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $r?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559774 1072 #uuid "04117d3f-552c-54f2-b117-fd9ff4546b57" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559774 1074 "Since the irradiance $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the radius of the spherical chamber, $r$, we must have\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $I=0.6\\,W/{m}^{2}$ when $r=3\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.6 & =\\frac{k}{{3}^{2}} \\\\ 0.6 & =\\frac{k}{9} \\\\ 0.6\\cdot 9 & =k \\\\ 5.4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{5.4}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559761 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559775 1069 "q-174417" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559775 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\n- $P:5\\,|\\,9$\n\n- $Q:\\sqrt{2}+0.2$\n\n- $R:\\sqrt{2}\\in R$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559775 1072 #uuid "0a9725fe-5d32-5217-81b5-21b1fce3c258" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559775 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options: \n\n- $P$ is a statement. This statement is *false* because $5$ does not divide $9.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\n- $R$ is a statement. This statement is *true* because $\\sqrt{2}$ is a real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559696 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559776 1069 "q-174379" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559776 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\n- $P:$ $0.5$ is a rational number.\n\n- $Q:$ $0.5$ is an irrational number.\n\n- $R:$ Divide $5$ by $10.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559776 1072 #uuid "0ac2c565-7f72-5deb-8186-0f592c4c5383" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559776 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options:\n\n- $P$ is a statement. This statement is *true* because $0.5=\\frac{5}{10},$ so it is a rational number.\n\n- $Q$ is a statement. This statement is *false* because $0.5$ is a rational number.\n\n- $R$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false. Rather, it is an instruction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559671 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559777 1069 "q-11627" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559777 1071 "![](images/20/20aaaae06d88f9105276f77bb65975e7d775c8fd15fbf4984d9b1889e73503dd.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space. Given that every outcome is equally likely, find $P(A\\cap B)$ to $2$ decimal places." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559777 1072 #uuid "0b138f98-60f4-5926-8616-f98d6e7b3754" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559777 1074 "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A\\cap B$ is represented by the area that is inside the overlap between the circles (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/c0/c0c7df273c9b71fc0286b03d7405cb8ca473d14b1020fc1ec15530879ddb8f1c.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A\\cap B$ is $15.$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n20+15+25+40=100.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B)=\\frac{15}{100}=\\frac{3}{20}=0.15\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559710 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559778 1069 "q-112994" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559778 1071 "What is $\\frac{5{h}^{2}-10h-75}{h+3}$ reduced to lowest terms?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559778 1072 #uuid "0c6bffa8-a78e-5e02-8975-6f49c316264d" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559778 1074 "The numerator can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{h}^{2}-10h-75=5({h}^{2}-2h-15)=5(h-5)(h+3).\n$$\n\nCanceling out any common factors that appear in the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{h}^{2}-10h-75}{h+3} & = \\\\ \\frac{5(h-5)(h+3)}{h+3} & = \\\\ \\frac{5(h-5)\\cancel{(h+3)}}{\\cancel{h+3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5(h-5)}{1} & = \\\\ 5h-25 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559664 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559779 1069 "q-174324" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559779 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559779 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P:$ A square has exactly four sides.\n\n- $Q:$ $7+2\\ge 10$\n\n- $R:$ $9$ is a prime number." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559779 1072 #uuid "0f8c15c2-cbc4-5d0f-bd4c-5f44e2f7b150" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559779 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate the truth value of each statement:\n\n- Statement $P$ is *true.* Indeed, A square has exactly four sides.\n\n- Statement $Q$ is *false* because $7+2=9≱10.$\n\n- Statement $R$ is *false* because $9$ is not a prime number (it is a composite number).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559657 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559780 1069 "q-96828" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559780 1071 "![](images/1b/1b124a0e4439f1b35affa5a38f175a151cbbad88affb12738585da8280bacb23.png)\n\nConsider the function $f:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The domain of $f$ is $\\{0,1\\}$\n\nII. The codomain of $f$ is $\\{0,1\\}$\n\nIII. The range of $f$ is $\\{0,1\\}$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559780 1072 #uuid "1110805d-d0df-5ae9-af4b-5eeeabf99273" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559780 1074 "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $f$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X=\\{0,1\\},$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y=\\{0,1,2,3\\},$\n\n- the range of $f$ is $\\{0,1\\},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"I and III only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559756 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559781 1069 "q-23553" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559781 1071 "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $4{x}^{2}=25.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559781 1072 #uuid "1bf2ec1b-375e-51fb-ba57-6d1d010ff509" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559781 1074 "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-25 & =0 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-(5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (2x-5)(2x+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n2x-5=0\\,\\text{or}\\,2x+5=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =0 & \\, & \\, & 2x+5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 & \\, & \\, & 2x & =-5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5}{2}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $x=\\pm \\frac{5}{2}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559782 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559713 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559782 1069 "q-72015" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559782 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559782 1072 #uuid "1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559782 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0-(-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,-1) & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{1}=1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,-1) & =-\\frac{1}{0-(-1)}=-\\frac{1}{1}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0).\n$$\n\nwhich is also undefined. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559661 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559783 1069 "q-116337" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559783 1071 "The intensity of radiation $I,$ in milliroentgen/hour ($\\text{mR}/\\text{h}$), spreading out from an X-ray device is inversely proportional to the **square** of the distance $d,$ measured in meters, between the device and the object being radiated. If a device that measures the radiation intensity registers $82\\,\\text{mR}/\\text{h}$ at a distance of $5\\,\\text{m}.$ What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $d?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559783 1072 #uuid "1ed2e1e9-66f4-55a8-9c62-03d8b636c4ac" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559783 1074 "Since the intensity $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the distance $d,$ we must have\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $82\\,\\text{mR}/\\text{h}$ when $d=5\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}82 & =\\frac{k}{{5}^{2}} \\\\ 82 & =\\frac{k}{25} \\\\ 82\\cdot 25 & =k \\\\ 2\\,050 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{2\\,050}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559714 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559784 1069 "q-45925" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559784 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559784 1071 "For an object moving in a straight line, its acceleration is the rate of change of its speed. What are the units of acceleration in terms of meters and seconds?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559784 1072 #uuid "1fe48b5d-2d75-5bc6-88de-325b6398cfcb" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559784 1074 "We *measure the change* in the speed, as *we change* the time. Consequently, any appropriate unit must take the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{unit of speed}}{\\text{unit of time}}.\n$$\n\nIn terms of meters and seconds, the unit for speed is $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}},$ and the unit for time is $\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the appropriate unit for acceleration is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}})}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nor, meters per square second." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559724 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559785 1069 "q-95404" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559785 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559785 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is diagonalizable\n\nII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u=\\alpha v$ for some scalar $\\alpha $\n\nIII. $A$ might have a complex eigenvalue" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559785 1072 #uuid "210e4b47-1e0a-56be-aa39-497b4081eae6" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559785 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, so, $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. So if the two eigenvalues correspond to different eigenvalues, we cannot have $u=\\alpha v$ for some scalar $\\alpha .$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559700 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559786 1069 "q-178587" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559786 1071 "$64\\times (-2)^{-3}=${{field-1}}" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559786 1072 #uuid "21de0665-fffe-50d8-acfd-284a6430dcc9" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559786 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that $(-2)^{-3}=\\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64\\times (-2)^{-3} & = \\\\ 64\\times \\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}} & = \\\\ 64\\times \\frac{1}{(-8)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{64}{8} & = \\\\ -(64÷8) & = \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559650 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559787 1069 "q-12785" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559787 1071 "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $4{k}^{2}-36=0.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559787 1072 #uuid "22229a6c-eb9f-551d-9867-ebd127134155" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559787 1074 "Before we start our normal process of factoring, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $4.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4{k}^{2}-36=4({k}^{2}-9)\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({k}^{2}-9) & =0 \\\\ 4({k}^{2}-{3}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4(k-3)(k+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nk-3=0\\,\\text{or}\\,k+3=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nk=3\\,\\text{or}\\,k=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=-3,3.$ We can write this even more concisely as $k=\\pm 3.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559723 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559788 1069 "q-106750" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559788 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559788 1072 #uuid "2ade0c9c-871d-5f78-8adc-bcd701d0727a" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559788 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second  equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-4.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2(-4) & =-10 \\\\ -{x}_{1} & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+(-4)=-2.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559750 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559789 1069 "q-105095" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559789 1071 "Let $b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find $-\\frac{1}{2}b.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559789 1072 #uuid "2af09a21-2c9f-5742-9701-8495f499a998" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559789 1074 "Multiplying each component of $b$ by $-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}b & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559665 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559790 1069 "q-96754" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559790 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set of rational numbers.\"*" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559790 1072 #uuid "33ee1a61-aa83-56a5-8209-eec63038ff68" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559790 1074 "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set of rational numbers\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:X\\to Q$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $X,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $Q.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559666 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559791 1069 "q-105094" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559791 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Find $2a.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559791 1072 #uuid "34bb1a50-1857-5b83-b60b-483ccca13301" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559791 1074 "Multiplying each component of $a$ by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & =2[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (-7) \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-3) \\\\ 2\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559722 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559792 1069 "q-139527" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559792 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559792 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $11,$ $9,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559792 1072 #uuid "352fc2e3-10d1-5d70-be93-5831359ed522" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559792 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $11,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=11\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{11}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559676 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559793 1069 "q-347273" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559793 1071 "![](images/12/12a9961800e0578de92b428e1a1a6ba5e10e25fe635a90f85c772f9bf73dc997.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $15\\,cm\\times 7\\,cm.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{cm}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height $y$ of the box that gives the maximum possible volume.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559793 1072 #uuid "35d621eb-cf33-51a5-9f2c-6f01d51e0a4e" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559793 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=15-2y,\\,z=7-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $15-2y>0$ and $7-2y>0$, hence $y<\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(15-2y)(y)(7-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-44{y}^{2}+105y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-88y+105,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-88y+105 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{35}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $\\frac{35}{6}$ since we already know that $0<y<\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{3}{2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-88.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{3}{2})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{3}{2}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the height of the box with the maximum volume is $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\text{ cm}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559684 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559794 1069 "q-96819" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559794 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559794 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $g:x↦1?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559794 1072 #uuid "366b227d-9c09-5249-bc0f-cb45c09620a0" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559794 1074 "The notation $g:x↦1$ means that the function $g$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $1$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\ng(x)=1.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559795 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559682 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559795 1069 "q-73943" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559795 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559795 1071 "Let\n$r=[\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ x \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $r\\cdot s=7$, find the value of $x.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559795 1072 #uuid "36b464cf-4c70-5aa3-a6c7-ba1498951d9c" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559795 1074 "Starting from the equation $r\\cdot s=7,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot s & =7 \\\\ (x-2)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot x+1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1 & =7 \\\\ x-2+6-3x-2+1 & =7 \\\\ -2x+3 & =7 \\\\ 2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x=-2.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559701 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559796 1069 "q-139756" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559796 1071 "$$\nR\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}={Q}^{T}\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559796 1072 #uuid "3a651338-245e-55fb-922d-e222a82f10f8" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559796 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ R\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $x$ and $b.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559740 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559797 1069 "q-14376" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559797 1071 "The point $P(-2,5)$ is reflected across the $x$-axis, shifted $3$ units down, and then reflected across the line $y=x.$ What is the resulting point?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559797 1072 #uuid "3b1c8849-8725-5033-99b9-7b44196735cf" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559797 1074 "Let's recall the functional representations of our three transformations:\n\n- A reflection in the $x$-axis can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,-y).\n$$\n\n- A shift of $3$ units down can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,y-3).\n$$\n\n- A reflection in the line $y=x$ can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(y,x).\n$$\n\nLet's apply the given transformations to the point $P$ using our functions:\n\nFirst, we apply the reflection. The image of the point $(-2,5)$ under the reflection is\n\n$$\n(-2,5)↦(-2,-5).\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we apply the shift. The image of the point $(-2,-5)$ under this shift is\n\n$$\n(-2,-5)↦(-2,-8).\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the reflection in the line $y=x.$ The image of the point $(-2,-8)$ under this reflection is\n\n$$\n(-2,-8)↦(-8,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(-8,-2).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559667 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559798 1069 "q-346784" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559798 1071 "![](images/4c/4cafc2891cfd957aad10e876d51757e8c6d7e84844927cb57ce1c2e704fe5b55.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is three times its width. Given that the box's volume is $972$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559798 1072 #uuid "3b6389e0-fab8-5e1d-bb5a-1e8d075edcaa" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559798 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $972$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 972 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=3x,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n972=3{x}^{2}y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y & =972 \\\\ y & =\\frac{324}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(3x)+2x(\\frac{324}{{x}^{2}})+2(\\frac{324}{{x}^{2}})(3x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+\\frac{648}{x}+\\frac{1944}{x} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+\\frac{2592}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=12x-\\frac{2592}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12x-\\frac{2592}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{3}-2592 & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{3} & =2592 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =216 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=6$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=12+\\frac{5184}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(6)=12+24=36>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(6)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=6$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(6) & =6(6)^{2}+\\frac{2592}{6} \\\\  & =648\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559729 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559799 1069 "q-175327" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559799 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559799 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n\nI. $z$ divided by $10.$\n\nII. $x\\times 3$\n\nIII. $y+3$ is an odd number." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559799 1072 #uuid "3bd80b9b-2655-5bf4-bdf9-f7428c6e7e13" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559799 1074 "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $z$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559704 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559800 1069 "q-95410" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559800 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559800 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$\n\nII. $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable\n\nIII. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559800 1072 #uuid "3fb92bee-a19d-5089-88c2-ed5488b77548" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559800 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559801 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559709 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559801 1069 "q-113009" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559801 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559801 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{wy+6y}{{w}^{2}+2w-24}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559801 1072 #uuid "414107e1-417e-5cee-adda-177a3b9f7f95" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559801 1074 "The numerator can be factored as $wy+6y=y(w+6)$ and the denominator can be factored as ${w}^{2}+2w-24=(w+6)(w-4).$ Canceling out any common factors that appear in the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{wy+6y}{{w}^{2}+2w-24} & =\\frac{y(w+6)}{(w+6)(w-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cancel{(w+6)}}{\\cancel{(w+6)}(w-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{w-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559693 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559802 1069 "q-178628" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559802 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559802 1071 "What is the solution to the equation $0.6y=-4.8?$\n\nThe solution is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559802 1072 #uuid "4143dbb0-9a1f-50d1-bc72-9b5c0cbd50d6" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559802 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.6y & =-4.8 \\\\ 10\\cdot (0.6y) & =10\\cdot (-4.8) \\\\ 6y & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y & =-48 \\\\ \\frac{6y}{6} & =\\frac{-48}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{6}y}{\\cancel{6}} & =-8 \\\\ y & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559681 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559803 1069 "q-35096" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559803 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559803 1071 "Let $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $c=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ Find $b+c.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559803 1072 #uuid "457dac21-00fa-5025-808b-b3e414ce1988" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559803 1074 "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has $5$ components as it lies in ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- The vector $c=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559698 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559804 1069 "q-117723" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559804 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559804 1071 "![](images/1e/1e6e5574bf3b15bd9d0c7592c24f20de7fae4d9d8110763383d7febb5582d166.png)\n\nHow many faces does the rectangular prism shown above have?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559804 1072 #uuid "45ea6d18-f63f-5746-8a3b-c9999a01036d" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559804 1074 "The rectangular prism has $6$ rectangular faces in total." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559730 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559805 1069 "q-174421" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559805 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\n- $P:\\{2,3,7\\}\\cap \\{3,5,6\\}=∅$\n\n- $Q:{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi $\n\n- $R:3$ is a prime number." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559805 1072 #uuid "4681538e-2de2-56f3-881c-562aebb342a4" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559805 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options: \n\n- $P$ is a statement. This statement is *false* because the intersection of the given sets is the set $\\{3\\}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\n- $R$ is a statement. This statement is *true* since $3$ is indeed a prime number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559806 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559705 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559806 1069 "q-73789" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559806 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559806 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-3x-2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559806 1072 #uuid "50aaea5b-0826-518a-8a27-2b0395848ac8" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559806 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(3x+2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+2{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-3(-\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =3(-\\frac{8}{27})+2(\\frac{4}{9})+3(\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{9}+\\frac{8}{9}+2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559807 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559677 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559807 1069 "q-44125" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559807 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559807 1071 "What are the coordinates of the minimum point of the function $y=(x+5)^{2}+3?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559807 1072 #uuid "52805580-1159-55ea-b90b-d6e5fe978449" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559807 1074 "Remember that the graph of $y=(x-h)^{2}+k$ is a translation of the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of $y=(x-h)^{2}+k$ is located at $(h,k).$\n\nHere, the parabola is translated $5$ units *left*, so we have $h=-5.$ The parabola is also translated $3$ units *up*, so we have $k=3.$ \n\n![](images/25/25221241ae83ee4bf78c712ea70d8e4cabea2cb569f59be3122412bbb2cae886.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(-5,3),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559808 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559643 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559808 1069 "q-96742" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559808 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559808 1071 "Which of the following functions has the codomain $R?$\n\nI. $p:[0,1]\\to R$\n\nII. $q:R\\to R$\n\nIII. $r:[0,1]\\to [0,1]$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559808 1072 #uuid "5328fc24-dd2c-524e-ade9-b248f99c64bf" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559808 1074 "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $[0,1],$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (including $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559753 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559809 1069 "q-61795" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559809 1071 "![](images/ce/ce2192c9d1a37853805dd948de4b417ca869c0412a5adac2056ec5e25d60a462.png)\n\nHow many vertices does the triangular prism shown above have?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559809 1072 #uuid "56054cd4-35fb-5c1d-9d3b-f750cda48405" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559809 1074 "The vertices of a polyhedron are the corners where edges meet.\n\nThe triangular prism has $6$ vertices in total, as shown below. \n\n![](images/9d/9ddb5284d9e08f0c2dcb3d5c901cfcdce93e909d6565fa95f24ee540c142d57b.png)" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559680 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559810 1069 "q-120355" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559810 1071 "Some customers in a pizzeria were asked about the type of pizza they prefer between spicy pizza and vegetarian pizza. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Prefers Spicy | Prefers  Vegetarian | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Children | $10$ | $22$ | $32$ |\n| Adults | $41$ | $27$ | $68$ |\n| Totals | $51$ | $49$ | $100$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen person is an adult or prefers spicy pizza (or both)." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559810 1072 #uuid "5c991f61-1cd6-5a73-ae3c-f2cc1cf3d784" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559810 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen person is an adult or prefers spicy pizza, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"Adults\"** or the column **\"Prefers Spicy\"**, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the row **\"Adults\"** or the column **\"Prefers Spicy\"** are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Prefers Spicy | Prefers Vegetarian | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Children | $10$ | $22$ | $32$ |\n| Adults | $41$ | $27$ | $68$ |\n| Totals | $51$ | $49$ | $100$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{10+41+27}{100}=\\frac{78}{100}=0.78.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559708 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559811 1069 "q-115600" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559811 1071 "Let $b$ be directly proportional to $g.$ Given that $b=98$ when $g=-7$, find the value of $b$ when $g=\\frac{31}{7}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559811 1072 #uuid "5ce15c8c-b8c7-513a-aedc-297e6604d6a3" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559811 1074 "Since $b$ varies directly with $g,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nb=kg.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $b=98$ when $g=-7.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}98 & =k\\cdot (-7) \\\\ \\frac{98}{(-7)} & =k \\\\ -14 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nb=-14g.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $g=\\frac{31}{7}$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =-14\\cdot \\frac{31}{7} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 31 \\\\  & =-62.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559812 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559717 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559812 1069 "q-51471" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559812 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559812 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3x+12}=\\frac{2}{x+4}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559812 1072 #uuid "600419ab-eff5-5dfa-9145-b6a621ba7867" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559812 1074 "First, note that $x=-4$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3x+12} & =\\frac{2}{x+4} \\\\ 2\\cdot (x+4) & =(3x+12)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2x+8 & =6x+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+8 & =6x+24 \\\\ 2x+8-24 & =6x+24-24 \\\\ 2x-16 & =6x \\\\ 2x-16-2x & =6x-2x \\\\ -16 & =4x \\\\ \\frac{-16}{4} & =\\frac{4x}{4} \\\\ -4 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-4$ cannot be a solution, the equation has no solutions." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559763 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559813 1069 "q-73995" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559813 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559813 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3+t \\\\ y=-1+2t \\\\ z=2+4t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:6x+4y+3z=88.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559813 1072 #uuid "63cc57b7-1fbb-5327-9f12-ff47fb613fec" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559813 1074 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+4y+3z & =88 \\\\ 6(-3+t)+4(-1+2t)+3(2+4t) & =88 \\\\ -18+6t-4+8t+6+12t & =88 \\\\ 26t & =104 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=4$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3+4=1 \\\\ y=-1+2(4)=7 \\\\ z=2+4(4)=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(1,7,18).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559649 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559814 1069 "q-132637" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559814 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559814 1071 "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y+z},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559814 1072 #uuid "6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559814 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when $y+z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y+z}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{(y+z)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+z)^{2} & \\ne 0 \\\\ y+z & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559679 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559815 1069 "q-95007" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559815 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559815 1071 "Consider the polynomials ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-{t}^{3},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFind $p(t)$ given that $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ and the coordinates of $p(t)$ relative to $B$ are $[p(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559815 1072 #uuid "71981b01-d421-5e25-8cb2-44a17e6d5470" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559815 1074 "Any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n[p(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =(-3)\\cdot (t-{t}^{3})+1\\cdot (2{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =-3t+3{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3} \\\\  & =-3t+2{t}^{2}+8{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559816 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559702 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559816 1069 "q-72017" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559816 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559816 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(2,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559816 1072 #uuid "73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559816 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(2,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(2,-1)=\\frac{2}{(-1)}=-2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-1) & =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(2,-1) & =-\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\frac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\frac{0}{1}=0,\n$$\n\n  and  ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(0,1)=\\frac{-0}{{1}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559654 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559817 1069 "q-47294" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559817 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559817 1071 "Roger surveyed $62$ Jefferson High School students. He asked them if they like biology and chemistry. His findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Like biology | Doesn't like biology | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Like chemistry | $25$ | $19$ | $44$ |\n| Doesn't like chemistry | $8$ | $10$ | $18$ |\n| Totals | $33$ | $29$ | $62$ |\n\nEdison High School has $500$ students. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same in both high schools, determine the number of Edison High School students who like biology or chemistry but not both. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559817 1072 #uuid "7775a984-c5ac-5540-8a1b-b2aae6fb0655" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559817 1074 "To estimate the number of students who like biology or chemistry but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"Like chemistry\"** or the column **\" Like biology\"**, but not in both, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | Like biology | Doesn't like biology | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Like chemistry | $25$ | $19$ | $44$ |\n| Doesn't like chemistry | $8$ | $10$ | $18$ |\n| Totals | $33$ | $29$ | $62$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(62),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{8+19}{62}=\\frac{27}{62}=0.435.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student likes biology or chemistry but not both.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of students of Edison High School who like biology or chemistry but not both, we multiply the total number of students in the Edison High School $(500)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =500\\cdot p \\\\  & =500\\cdot 0.435 \\\\  & =217.5 \\\\  & =218,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559669 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559683 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559759 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559770 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559818 1069 "q-336907" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559818 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559818 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-2x(t)-3 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559818 1072 #uuid "7b1e877f-fd37-5577-a12f-56b4c019e4b2" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559818 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4y-2x-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4y-2x-3)=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4y-2x-3)=4 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3)=-2y+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & 4 \\\\ 2x & -2y+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1)-2 & 4 \\\\ 2(1) & -2(1)+3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559819 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559737 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559819 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559758 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559819 1069 "q-214839" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559819 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559819 1071 "Using the elimination method, eliminate the variable $p$ and find the solution $(p,q)$ to the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4p+q=-24 \\\\ -4p+7q=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559819 1072 #uuid "7d0d1a49-c8c0-5ac4-a633-472aeac1d8a8" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559819 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $p$ in the first equation is $4,$ and the coefficient of $p$ in the second equation is $-4.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $p,$ and solve the resulting equation for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{4p}+q & =-24 \\\\ -\\cancel{4p}+7q & =24 \\\\ (q+7q) & =(-24+24) \\\\ 8q & =0 \\\\ q & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $q=0.$ However, we still need to find $p.$ To do this, we substitute $q=0$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p+q & =-24 \\\\ 4p+(0) & =-24 \\\\ 4p & =-24 \\\\ p & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-6,\\,q=0,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,-\\,6,0\\,).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559749 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559820 1069 "q-176872" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559820 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬[¬(¬(¬C)∧D)].$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559820 1072 #uuid "7d9b9daf-a686-5f9d-9e9b-43c577599d8d" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559820 1074 "Two negations in a row cancel each other out. So, we can simplify $¬(¬C)$ to $C.$ \n\nAs a result of this, the statement simplifes to \n\n$$\n¬[¬(C∧D)].\n$$\n\nThen, we can simplify $¬[¬(C∧D)]]$ to $C∧D.$\n\nTherefore, the given statement can be simplified to \n\n$$\nC∧D.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559651 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559821 1069 "q-3488" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559821 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559821 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{3{n}^{2}+15n+18}{{n}^{2}+4n+4}?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559821 1072 #uuid "80a74124-ec47-55a3-9e24-4bd2a521aa8c" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559821 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator, canceling out any common factors that appear in both.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{n}^{2}+15n+18}{{n}^{2}+4n+4} & =\\frac{3({n}^{2}+5n+6)}{{n}^{2}+4n+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(n+3)(n+2)}{(n+2)(n+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(n+3)\\cancel{(n+2)}}{(n+2)\\cancel{(n+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(n+3)}{n+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3n+9}{n+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559768 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559822 1069 "q-38040" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559822 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559822 1071 "For which of the following polynomials is $(x+3)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-8x+3$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-x-3$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x-2$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559822 1072 #uuid "81cebe4e-1d65-5357-b789-c0fc16951ab5" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559822 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=-3$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-3$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =2(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-8(-3)+3 \\\\  & =2(-27)+3(9)+24+3 \\\\  & =-54+27+24+3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-3$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-(-3)-3 \\\\  & =-27+3(9)+3-3 \\\\  & =-27+27+3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-3$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+2(-3)^{2}-(-3)-2 \\\\  & =-27+2(9)+3-2 \\\\  & =-27+18+3-2 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559726 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559823 1069 "q-214915" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559823 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559823 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-p+7q=-4 \\\\ 10p-9q=-21\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe variable $p$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1\\. Multiply the first equation by $10$ and obtain\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}-10p+70q & =-40.\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Add the multiplied equation to the second equation to get\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$=-61.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559823 1072 #uuid "81d71337-d62e-547f-9ba2-a1325854394f" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559823 1074 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $10$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-p+7q=-4\\,)\\,\\times (10) \\\\ 10p-9q=-21\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-10p+70q=-40 \\\\ 10p-9q=-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{10p}+70q & =-40 \\\\ \\cancel{10p}-9q & =-21 \\\\ (70q-9q) & =(-40-21) \\\\ 61q & =-61\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559697 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559824 1069 "q-78940" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559824 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559824 1071 "$|-\\frac{4{c}^{2}}{7}|=$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559824 1072 #uuid "8404264d-3a64-5581-ad4c-0afe9c6e6c44" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559824 1074 "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|\\frac{4{c}^{2}}{7}|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{4{c}^{2}}{7}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|4{c}^{2}|}{|7|} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|{c}^{2}|}{|7|} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|{c}^{2}|}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${c}^{2}\\ge 0$ for any real $c,$ we have that $|{c}^{2}|={c}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4|{c}^{2}|}{7} & =\\frac{4{c}^{2}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559652 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559825 1069 "q-72028" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559825 1071 "Given that $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=-\\frac{2}{y},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $h$ exist." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559825 1072 #uuid "88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559825 1074 "First, notice that $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}})$ is defined only when $xz>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $h$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{h}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${h}_{x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${h}_{y}=-\\frac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${h}_{z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $h$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{h}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cap \\,\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559668 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559826 1069 "q-347083" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559826 1071 "![](images/d6/d67ba70d92807f0a7298fb8222252a047f7eec03a26878a5aa95b0fb82a7335c.png)\n\nA box with no lid whose depth $z$ is $3$ times its height $y$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$2$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$1$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $972\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559826 1072 #uuid "88d64cef-5be0-59d0-8f76-951d4350e51b" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559826 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2xz+1\\cdot 2xy+1\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =2xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $972\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 972 & =x\\cdot z\\cdot (\\frac{z}{3}) \\\\ 972 & =\\frac{1}{3}x{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x{z}^{2} & =972 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2916}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2\\,(\\frac{2916}{{z}^{2}})\\,z+2\\,(\\frac{2916}{{z}^{2}})\\,(\\frac{z}{3})+2\\,(\\frac{z}{3})\\,z \\\\  & =\\frac{5832}{z}+\\frac{1944}{z}+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7776}{z}+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=\\frac{4}{3}z-\\frac{7776}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}z-\\frac{7776}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{z}^{3} & =7776 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =5832 \\\\ z & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=18$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{15552}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(18)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}=4>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(18)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=18$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=18$ units." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559711 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559827 1069 "q-117217" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559827 1071 "![](images/af/afc68dbb5c336cff31e75f5ed92dd853c8fc7ec43e8fa66539d27d83b9e2070f.png)\n\nA box with no lid and a square base has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$3$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$5$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $2\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $x$ for the box that has the smallest production cost?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559827 1072 #uuid "8973c685-fd2c-51e0-89ff-171e4339d4cb" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559827 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =3{x}^{2}+5\\cdot 4xy \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+20xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $2\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot x\\cdot y \\\\ 2 & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =3{x}^{2}+20xy \\\\ C & =3{x}^{2}+20x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ C & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{40}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(x)=6x-\\frac{40}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 6x-\\frac{40}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3} & =40 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(x)=6+\\frac{80}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}})=6+12=18>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}}$ is indeed a minimum of $C(x).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559678 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559828 1069 "q-96761" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559828 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set of real numbers to the set of rational numbers.\"*" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559828 1072 #uuid "915687d1-7d3d-5688-b25a-20c50fc27b30" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559828 1074 "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of real numbers to the set of rational numbers\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:R\\to Q$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $R,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $Q.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559762 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559829 1069 "q-93184" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559829 1071 "![](images/08/08824dd380c2db6f76064b6602604fc20a60cf7a07525c830eb6473eb08fe1dd.png)\n\nHow many edges does the heptagonal pyramid shown above have?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559829 1072 #uuid "9184a58a-ba3f-54e5-a4d1-d4f95b399278" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559829 1074 "Edges are straight line segments that connect the vertices of a polyhedron. \n\nThe heptagonal pyramid has $14$ edges in total, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9b5ee9ae6a6d1e83bd71d483956a9b713bce6a731f42b9b878d7c11e6014cdef.png)" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559754 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559830 1069 "q-43567" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559830 1071 "Find $\\text{LCM}(18,30).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559830 1072 #uuid "919304f4-9784-5406-8c58-3a53fea4edc4" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559830 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $18:$ \n\n![](images/9e/9e7bddde8a287f6224b3e79f9fdacc3ac22d6db5a021b85df287e5e5fa321932.png)\n\n \nTherefore, $18=2\\cdot {3}^{2}.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $30:$\n\n![](images/31/312d8c33355fb36260cce172ab20bf907637528d0a5d04dc4dd1eceb59d765dd.png)\n\n \nTherefore, $30=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ 30 & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $18$ and $30$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(18,30)=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 5=90.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559752 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559831 1069 "q-36525" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559831 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}},$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559831 1072 #uuid "93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559831 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ y & >{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{-2x}{2(y-{x}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ y & >{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559720 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559832 1069 "q-104941" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559832 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $h(x)=2{x}^{3}-3x?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559832 1072 #uuid "97bd3e47-4a7d-51fe-87e4-ccb53cfbd7e2" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559832 1074 "The notation $h(x)=2{x}^{3}-3x$ means that the function $h$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $2{x}^{3}-3x$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nh:x↦2{x}^{3}-3x.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559718 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559833 1069 "q-47137" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559833 1071 "A randomly selected group of students from a high school were asked about their favorite sport between baseball, football, basketball, or soccer. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Baseball | Football | Basketball | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $10$ | $19$ | $10$ | $12$ | $51$ |\n| Boys | $10$ | $13$ | $20$ | $6$ | $49$ |\n| Totals | $20$ | $32$ | $30$ | $18$ | $100$ |\n\nLet $F$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the high school is a **\"Girl\"** whose favorite sport is **\"Football\"**, and $S$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the high school is a **\"Girl\"** whose favorite sport is **\"Soccer\"**. Estimate $P(F\\cup S).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559833 1072 #uuid "9952b961-280f-5d1f-a0eb-bf2ca705ef14" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559833 1074 "First, note that $F\\cup S$ is the event that a randomly selected student in the high school is a girl whose favorite sport is football or a girl whose favorite sport is soccer. \n\nTo estimate the probability of $F\\cup S,$ we\n\n- add up the numbers in the columns **\"Football\"** or **\"Soccer\"** and the row **\"Girls\"**, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the columns **\"Football\"** or **\"Soccer\"** and the row **\"Girls\"** are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Baseball | Football | Basketball | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $10$ | $19$ | $10$ | $12$ | $51$ |\n| Boys | $10$ | $13$ | $20$ | $6$ | $49$ |\n| Totals | $20$ | $32$ | $30$ | $18$ | $100$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(F\\cup S)=\\frac{19+12}{100}=\\frac{31}{100}=0.31.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559653 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559834 1069 "q-73947" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559834 1071 "Let $r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ {x}^{4} \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $r\\cdot s=0$ and $x<0$, find the value of $x.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559834 1072 #uuid "99569175-b9f3-5c01-983c-724e408918e3" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559834 1074 "Starting from the equation $r\\cdot s=0,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot s & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot {x}^{4}+0\\cdot 8+x\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ -3+2{x}^{2}+0-x & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-x-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-1)\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4(2)(-3)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{25}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get two solutions, ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=-1.$ Since we need $x<0$, our final answer is $x=-1$ only." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559769 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559835 1069 "q-120409" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559835 1071 "The time $t$, in minutes ($min$), it takes for an ice cream to melt is inversely proportional to the environment's temperature $T,$ in degrees Celsius (${}^{∘}C$). If it takes $10\\,min$ for an ice cream to melt at  ${30}^{∘}C,$ how much time it takes for the ice cream to melt at ${20}^{∘}C?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559835 1072 #uuid "99714685-f61c-5dfd-b220-40d2fda79ac5" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559835 1074 "The time of melting $t$ is inversely proportional to the temperature $T,$ so we have\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{k}{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that it takes $10\\,min$ for an ice cream to melt at ${30}^{∘}C.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{k}{30} \\\\ 10\\cdot 30 & =k \\\\ 300 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{300}{T}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the time it takes for the ice cream to melt at ${20}^{∘}C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{300}{20}=15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time it takes for the ice cream to melt when $T={20}^{∘}C$ is $t=15\\,min.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559646 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559836 1069 "q-105106" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559836 1071 "Let $r=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ What is $r\\cdot s?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559836 1072 #uuid "99f979f5-50b6-5282-8195-712172f0dd94" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559836 1074 "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $r=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\n- The vector $s=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has $5$ components as it lies in ${R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559738 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559837 1069 "q-11631" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559837 1071 "At a band audition, there are $50$ singers. There are $27$ country singers, $23$ pop singers, and $6$ country and pop singers. Draw a Venn diagram representing this sample space. If a singer is selected at random among the $50$ singers, what is the probability that the singer sings country or pop music (or both)? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559837 1072 #uuid "9ac83742-fbb3-5922-b2ad-70bce502b290" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559837 1074 "Let us call $A$ the event \"the singer sings country music\" and $B$ the event \"the singer sings pop music.\"\n\nNote that there are $6$ singers singing both country and pop music. So, we draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $6$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of singers who *only* sing country music is\n\n$$\n27-6=21,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $21$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of singers who *only* sing pop music is\n\n$$\n23-6=17,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $17$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A.$\n\nThe total number of singers who sing country or pop music is\n\n$$\n6+21+17=44,\n$$\n\nand there are $50$ singers in total, so the number of remaining singers is\n\n$$\n50-44=6.\n$$\n\nTo represent these $6$ singers who did not sing country or pop music, we write the number $6$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nTherefore, our Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](images/02/0240b83c3de5c8c3f3b1e91d3b9c8ae2899f0b2a18611d7e70b6dbf71f23e25b.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to singers that sing country or pop music (or both). The probability that a randomly selected singer is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\frac{44}{50}=0.88.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559766 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559838 1069 "q-81404" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559838 1071 "$11$ is **not** a prime number, or $15$ is **not** divisible by $5.$\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- $P:11\\text{ is a prime number}$\n\n- $Q:15\\text{ is divisible by }5$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559838 1072 #uuid "9c84d136-aecb-53d5-b6db-0a2d449203b9" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559838 1074 "The sentence\n\n$11$ is **not** a prime number \n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $¬P.$ \n\nThe sentence\n\n$15$ is **not** divisible by $5$ \n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the sentence\n\n$11$ is **not** a prime number, or $15$ is **not** divisible by $5$\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $¬P∨¬Q.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559699 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559727 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559748 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559839 1069 "q-208822" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559839 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+5y=9 \\\\ 2x-y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe variable $y$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1\\. Multiply the second equation by $5$ and obtain\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}10x-5y=5.\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$=14.$\n\n$\\,$\nHence, the solution to the system of equations is $($\n{{field-2}}$\\,,$\n{{field-3}}\n$).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559839 1072 #uuid "a91b43fb-d787-5c95-8cec-baa3db0b3a5b" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559839 1074 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $5$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+5y=9 \\\\ (\\,2x-y=1\\,)\\,\\times (5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+5y=9 \\\\ 10x-5y=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+\\cancel{5y} & =9 \\\\ 10x-\\cancel{5y} & =5 \\\\ (-3x+10x) & =(9+5) \\\\ 7x & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=2.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =1 \\\\ 2(2)-y & =1 \\\\ 4-y & =1 \\\\ -y & =-3 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,2,3\\,).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559687 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559840 1069 "q-35098" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559840 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k+2\\end{aligned}].$ If $u\\cdot v=0$, find the value of $k.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559840 1072 #uuid "ac43b1b5-6764-5241-bdc0-c6b7a39ba60f" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559840 1074 "Starting from the equation $u\\cdot v=0,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $k$ and then solve for $k$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot k+3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-5)+6\\cdot (k+2) & =0 \\\\ k+12-10+6k+12 & =0 \\\\ 7k+14 & =0 \\\\ 7k & =-14 \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $k=-2.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559689 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559841 1069 "q-174322" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559841 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P:$ $2>6$\n\n- $Q:$ $10$ is an integer number.\n\n- $R:$ $7$ is a composite number." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559841 1072 #uuid "ae278ad9-7ac4-5180-982a-a8a5385a7ed1" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559841 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate the truth value of each statement:\n\n- Statement $P$ is *false* because $2≯6.$\n\n- Statement $Q$ is *true.* Indeed, $10$ is an integer number.\n\n- Statement $R$ is *false* because $7$ is not a composite number (it is a prime number).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559744 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559842 1069 "q-61801" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559842 1071 "![](images/a9/a9ed0df71b7e3948f6cd37610b1a0d09bf6efcc4561aa0b028a1e48ca8fc7a0a.png)\n\nHow many vertices does the hexagonal prism shown above have?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559842 1072 #uuid "ae8fc30f-28c7-5b20-9ff5-bcddd8703aac" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559842 1074 "The vertices of a polyhedron are the corners where edges meet.\n\nThe rectangular prism has $12$ vertices in total, as shown below. \n\n![](images/64/640e513f88583e384d7bd38add70f8df25042db1b1dcc14184d89cb02bc811c3.png)" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559736 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559843 1069 "q-102847" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559843 1071 "Which of the following is a mathematical statement?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559843 1072 #uuid "ae9ba4f6-2cb2-5711-bd11-5c1b1d70436a" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559843 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false. \n\nOf the given sentences, the only sentence that is either true or false is \"A rectangle has four angles.\" (This sentence is true.)\n\nAll the other sentences are neither true nor false." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559655 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559844 1069 "q-104951" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559844 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559844 1071 "![](images/6a/6ac4933860ceea29b5892d1ea38685dd7d7fbb5e1a6a48cda45b48151b35aeb1.png)\n\nConsider the function $g:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The domain of $g$ is $\\{1,2,3\\}$\n\nII. The codomain of $g$ is $\\{1,2,3\\}$\n\nIII. The range of $g$ is $\\{1,2,3\\}$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559844 1072 #uuid "af103ef7-b89b-5289-9d5d-02c1e186f66e" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559844 1074 "The notation $g:X\\to Y$ means that the function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $g$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X=\\{1,2,3\\},$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y=\\{1,2,3,4\\},$\n\n- the range of $g$ is $\\{1,2,3\\},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"I and III only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559644 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559845 1069 "q-105101" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559845 1071 "Let $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}].$ Find $2v+\\frac{1}{4}w.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559845 1072 #uuid "b2fb6e56-ff2e-5614-a5dd-38bf8fc25970" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559845 1074 "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has $5$ components as it lies in ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- The vector $w=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559662 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559694 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559846 1069 "q-335101" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559846 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x(t))+{y}^{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559846 1072 #uuid "b3908e50-0fb9-59ae-a2c0-f103ab5a2672" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559846 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2})=\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)=2xy,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0)(0) & {0}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 0-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559715 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559847 1069 "q-45182" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559847 1071 "![](images/46/46387d95d8b385f618da53c48284b323bed69e547a2e9894294f03aac7fa104e.png)\n\nThe triangle shown above is rotated by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the origin and then reflected across the $x$-axis. What is the resulting triangle?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559847 1072 #uuid "b4059559-692f-5333-8d82-5fcea28a66c0" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559847 1074 "Let's apply the given transformation.\n\n- Rotating the triangle by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the origin gives the following:\n\n![](images/e8/e858bc501573ce7290ae6c2c0601d1c5a03d76a44a332ac8a5588e027bc0eacc.png)\n\n- Next, we take the last result and reflect it across the $x$-axis. This gives the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6eaad9b0fc67babd22039d3c615789babd7469fc3e0822e4f585138750a6e3db.png)" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559663 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559848 1069 "q-5437" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559848 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n1,\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{9}{4},\\,…\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559848 1072 #uuid "b49e8f9b-353c-5ece-9748-47abc8dc5438" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559848 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=1$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2},$ so we can find the common ratio, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{1}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\frac{3}{2},$  we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559645 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559849 1069 "q-170877" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559849 1071 "Suppose $v$ is directly proportional to $u.$ Given that $v=-12$ when $u=3$, find the value of $v$ when $u=5.${{field-1}}." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559849 1072 #uuid "b590fcf1-ec20-533b-be69-95e4b245f98f" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559849 1074 "Since $v$ varies directly with $u,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nv=ku.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $v=-12$ when $u=3.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =k\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{(-12)}{3} & =k \\\\ -4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nv=-4u.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $u=5$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-4\\cdot 5=-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559733 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559850 1069 "q-121179" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559850 1071 "In a school, a survey conducted on the $40$ students shows that $17$ students traveled to school by train, $23$ students traveled by bus, and $10$ students traveled by both train and bus. \n\nIf $T$ represents the event that a student travels by train, and $B$ represents the event that a student travels by bus, which of the following diagrams gives the correct Venn diagram for the corresponding sample space?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559850 1072 #uuid "b91a0507-397b-5914-a663-1432f07abc1f" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559850 1074 "Note that there are $10$ students who traveled by both train and bus. We draw two overlapping circles representing the events $T$ and $B$ and write the number $10$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who traveled by train *only*  is\n\n$$\n17-10=7,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $7$ in the part of circle $T$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of students who traveled by bus *only*  is\n\n$$\n23-10=13,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $13$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $T.$\n\nThere are $40$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is \n\n$$\n40-7-10-13=10.\n$$\n\nTo represent the $10$ remaining students, we write the number $10$ outside of the circles $T$ and $B.$\n\nFinally, our Venn diagram looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a5/a511835f1ecf6a7f3991f8ebb9ed14339ebfab4cb41aa06363502df5abf03719.png)" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559725 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559851 1069 "q-117054" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559851 1071 "![](images/2c/2cfba1072b37cd316349997201ada6e67af702926214abec22a1ef5ae6543c5a.png)\n\nConsider a box with a square base with side lengths $x$ and height $y,$ as shown above. The volume of the box is $125$ cubic units. Given that the surface area of the box has been minimized, find the value of $x.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559851 1072 #uuid "bae7b49c-3856-514a-a9ab-b99ea914cf24" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559851 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2{x}^{2}+4xy\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is exactly $125$ cubic units. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot x\\cdot y \\\\ 125 & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =125 \\\\ y & =\\frac{125}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2{x}^{2}+4xy \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+4x\\cdot \\frac{125}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+\\frac{500}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=4x-\\frac{500}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x-\\frac{500}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{500}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 4{x}^{3} & =500 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=4+\\frac{1\\,000}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=4+8=12>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed a minimum of $S(x).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the smallest possible surface area has side length $x=5$ units." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559673 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559852 1069 "q-86474" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559852 1071 "Which of the following is a mathematical statement?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559852 1072 #uuid "c043e0c4-50de-5146-885c-1db3a041b406" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559852 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false. \n\nOf the given sentences, the only sentence that is either true or false is \"$3$ is a positive number.\" (This sentence is true.)\n\nAll the other sentences are neither true nor false." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559751 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559853 1069 "q-112376" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559853 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+x-6?$\n\nI. $x=3$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559853 1072 #uuid "c04c2ae6-0944-57e5-bf4e-7441e7fccca7" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559853 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=3$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ={3}^{2}+(3)-6 \\\\  & =9+3-6 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=3$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x-3)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x-3)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559658 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559854 1069 "q-120043" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559854 1071 "![](images/c5/c5f197f52df26da43b27b2b142f25462570828996ea28154f865bac2e871e972.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space. Given that every outcome is equally likely, find $P(A\\cup B)$ to $2$ decimal places." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559854 1072 #uuid "c12c5e9b-72e8-5854-9bf8-a8cf7ba5e8c9" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559854 1074 "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A\\cup B$ is represented by the area that is inside both circles (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/ad/ad51768e560488687b0dfbd573f71136f7863106d0a830828878bc5488b05daf.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A\\cup B$ is\n\n$$\n6+1+4=11.\n$$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n6+1+4+12=23.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\frac{11}{23}≈0.48\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559674 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559855 1069 "q-139496" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559855 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Any $5\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nII. Any $3\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. The singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ are negative." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559855 1072 #uuid "c14fd620-27ea-593f-816e-9c2134638c65" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559855 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 5}\\times {A}_{5\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 3}\\times {A}_{3\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559656 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559856 1069 "q-3131" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559856 1071 "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $49{j}^{2}-64=0.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559856 1072 #uuid "c2c62744-4e6e-5285-9246-bd3c3e1b0773" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559856 1074 "Using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49{j}^{2}-64 & =0 \\\\ (7j)^{2}-{8}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (7j-8)(7j+8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n7j-8=0\\,\\text{or}\\,7j+8=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7j-8 & =0 & \\, & \\, & 7j+8 & =0 \\\\ 7j & =8 & \\, & \\, & 7j & =-8 \\\\ j & =\\frac{8}{7} & \\, & \\, & j & =-\\frac{8}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $j=-\\frac{8}{7},\\frac{8}{7}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $j=\\pm \\frac{8}{7}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559685 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559857 1069 "q-51850" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559857 1071 "If $z$ varies directly with $w$ and $z=72$ when $w=9$, find the relationship between $w$ and $z.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559857 1072 #uuid "c6b59906-d6e5-5bad-9d0f-b7c18e490304" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559857 1074 "Since $z$ varies directly with $w,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nz=kw.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that $z=72$ when $w=9$ to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =9\\cdot k \\\\ \\frac{72}{9} & =k \\\\ 8 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nz=8w.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559771 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559858 1069 "q-33977" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559858 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨4,1,6⟩+t⟨2,6,0⟩,\\,Π:r\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩=-32.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559858 1072 #uuid "c9dd16b9-4054-5136-a332-5517b98207e0" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559858 1074 "Let's write the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨4+2t,1+6t,6⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨4+2t,1+6t,6⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ & =-32 \\\\ ⟨4+2t,1+6t,6⟩\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ & =-32 \\\\ -24(4+2t)+8(1+6t)+6\\cdot 6 & =-32 \\\\ -96-48t+8+48t+36 & =-32 \\\\ -52 & =-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has no solution. Therefore, we conclude that the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ do not intersect." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559721 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559859 1069 "q-52004" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559859 1071 "For a fixed temperature, the pressure $P$ of a gas varies inversely with the volume $V$ that it occupies. When $V=3\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ the pressure is $P=2$ megapascal ($\\text{MPa}$). What will be the pressure if the gas is compressed to a container with volume $V=2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559859 1072 #uuid "cb89cc75-091f-5263-a465-e0004e0e6c85" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559859 1074 "The pressure $P$ is inversely proportional to the volume $V,$ so we have\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{k}{V},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the volume is $V=3\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ the pressure is $P=2$ megapascal ($\\text{MPa}$). We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{k}{3} \\\\ 2\\cdot 3 & =k \\\\ 6 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{6}{V}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the pressure when the volume is $V=2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{6}{2}=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pressure of the gas when $V=2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}$ is $3\\,\\text{MPa}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559648 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559860 1069 "q-121180" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559860 1071 "In a class of $30$ students, $18$ have black hair, $9$ have blue eyes and $7$ have both black hair and blue eyes. Draw a Venn diagram representing this sample space. If a student is selected at random among the $30$ students, what is the probability that the student has black hair or blue eyes? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559860 1072 #uuid "cc44bf24-a689-5cdc-ad9e-c41be1cff05a" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559860 1074 "Let us call $A$ the event \"the student has black hair\" and $B$ the event \"the student has blue eyes.\"\n\nNote that there are $7$ students who have both black hair and blue eyes. So, we draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $7$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who *only* have black hair is\n\n$$\n18-7=11,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $11$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of students who *only* have blue eyes is\n\n$$\n9-7=2,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $2$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A.$\n\nThere are $30$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is \n\n$$\n30-11-7-2=10.\n$$\n\nTo represent these $10$ students that have not black hair or blue eyes, we write the number $10$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nTherefore, our Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](images/fa/fab4a1a0244dc20c692101b35bac04ca9e73f4458a709d2507ba8d9a21345109.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to students that have black hair or blue eyes. The probability that a randomly selected student is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\frac{11+7+2}{30}=\\frac{20}{30}≈0.67,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559703 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559707 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559861 1069 "q-214764" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559861 1071 "Using the elimination method, eliminate the variable $x$ and find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y=45 \\\\ -5x-7y=-70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559861 1072 #uuid "cd2697ea-50eb-59a5-a69d-76e0004cb7aa" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559861 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $5,$ and the coefficient of $x$ in the second equation is $-5.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $x,$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{5x}+2y & =45 \\\\ -\\cancel{5x}-7y & =-70 \\\\ (2y-7y) & =(45-70) \\\\ -5y & =-25 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=5.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2y & =45 \\\\ 5x+2(5) & =45 \\\\ 5x+10 & =45 \\\\ 5x & =35 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=7,\\,y=5,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,7,5\\,).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559747 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559862 1069 "q-35120" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559862 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find $u+2v.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559862 1072 #uuid "cdc2cbf3-48b6-588f-b958-c891b668d712" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559862 1074 "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has $3$ components as it lies in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559672 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559863 1069 "q-105096" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559863 1071 "Let $c=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\frac{1}{4}c.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559863 1072 #uuid "ceec6370-9876-5337-a51f-c0a5305aac98" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559863 1074 "Multiplying each component of $c$ by $\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}c & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 20 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-8) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559719 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559864 1069 "q-112468" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559864 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-3x+2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(2x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(2x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559864 1072 #uuid "cef78ed2-9af9-556a-8a9d-05a7101331ab" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559864 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(2x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{1}{2}) & ={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}-3(-\\frac{1}{2})+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559765 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559865 1069 "q-173721" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559865 1071 "Which of the following statements are false?\n\n- $P:$ $\\sqrt{5}$ is a real number.\n\n- $Q:$ $10$ is divisible by $3.$\n\n- $R:$ $2+3\\le 1$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559865 1072 #uuid "d4bf832a-fa7c-5bf9-96bc-b8972c38ecca" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559865 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate the truth value of each statement:\n\n- Statement $P$ is *true.* Indeed, $\\sqrt{5}$ is a real number.\n\n- Statement $Q$ is *false* because $10$ is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement $R$ is *false* because $2+3=5≰1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ and $R$ only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559760 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559866 1069 "q-102854" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559866 1071 "What is the truth value of the following mathematical statement?\n\n$\\,$ Half of $10$ is $5.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559866 1072 #uuid "d625ae65-2877-56d1-acca-93794ab5d50e" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559866 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nThis statement is true because the $10÷2=5.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559692 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559867 1069 "q-120744" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559867 1071 "A group of randomly selected customers from a certain insurance company was asked about the type of vehicle driven and whether they received a speeding ticket in the last year. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | SUV | Sport car | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Speeding ticket | $80$ | $5$ | $85$ |\n| No speeding ticket | $20$ | $5$ | $25$ |\n| Totals | $100$ | $10$ | $110$ |\n\nThere are $220$ drivers in another insurance company. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same for both companies, estimate the number of drivers in the second insurance company that drive SUV or received a speeding ticket, but not both." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559867 1072 #uuid "d65fd340-5d3c-5485-87ce-64ffa546bd48" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559867 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected driver drives the SUV or received a speeding ticket but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"Speeding ticket\"** or the column **\"SUV\"**, but not in both, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | SUV | Sport car | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Speeding ticket | $80$ | $5$ | $85$ |\n| No speeding ticket | $20$ | $5$ | $25$ |\n| Totals | $100$ | $10$ | $110$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(110),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{20+5}{110}=\\frac{5}{22}.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected driver drives an SUV or received a speeding ticket but not both.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of drivers in the second company that drive SUVs or received a speeding ticket, but not both, we multiply the total number of drivers in the company $(220)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =220\\cdot p \\\\  & =220\\cdot \\frac{5}{22} \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559743 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559868 1069 "q-73925" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559868 1071 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]$, then $-2v=$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559868 1072 #uuid "dc18df10-64c6-5957-81d9-6515439cccbe" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559868 1074 "Multiplying each component of $v$ by $-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2v & =-2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\ -2\\cdot 3 \\\\ -2\\cdot 0 \\\\ -2\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559706 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559869 1069 "q-174325" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559869 1071 "Which of the following statements are false?\n\n- $P:$ $\\sqrt{2}=1$\n\n- $Q:$ ${3}^{2}=9$\n\n- $R:$ $8$ is a composite number." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559869 1072 #uuid "dd39dbfa-f77c-5216-9581-0acf72d83216" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559869 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate the truth value of each statement:\n\n- Statement $P$ is *false* because $\\sqrt{2}\\ne 1.$\n\n- Statement $Q$ is *true.* Indeed, ${3}^{2}=9.$\n\n- Statement $R$ is *true.* Indeed, $8$ is a composite number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559688 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559870 1069 "q-95014" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559870 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559870 1072 #uuid "df9025d6-fcec-59a9-b2fb-091fd502413d" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559870 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-6 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(-2) & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+(-2)=0.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559757 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559871 1069 "q-148287" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559871 1071 "Peter pays $\\frac{3}{5}$ of a dollar for $\\frac{1}{15}$ of a pound of pumpkin seeds. How much should he pay for $3$ pounds of seeds?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559871 1072 #uuid "e0a4a8a4-751d-5872-ad1c-8d3477e4c365" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559871 1074 "To determine how much Peter should pay for $3$ pounds of seeds, we first find the unit rate of dollars per pound and then multiply it by $3$ pounds.\n\nTo find the unit rate of dollars per pound, we divide the number of dollars, $\\frac{3}{5},$ by the number of pounds, $\\frac{1}{15},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{1}{15}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{1}{15} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{15}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 15}{5\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{5} \\\\  & =45÷5 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $9$ dollars per pound. To determine how much Peter should pay for $3$ pounds, we multiply the unit rate by $3$ pounds and get\n\n$$\n9\\times 3=27.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Peter must pay $27$ dollars for $3$ pounds of pumpkin seeds." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559764 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559872 1069 "q-109927" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559872 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda -7)^{3}(\\lambda +4)^{2}(\\lambda -9)^{9},$ the rank of $(D-9I)^{2}$ equals $11,$ and the rank of $(D-9I)^{3}$ equals $8.$ If a basis of ${R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =9?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559872 1072 #uuid "e1e17bba-2392-58cc-bb67-1a8adce57d23" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559872 1074 "For the given matrix $D$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =9,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(D-9I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(D-9I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ must be a $14\\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(D-9I)^{2}$ equals $11,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{2}=14-11=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(D-9I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}$ of dimension $3$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(D-9I)^{3}$ equals $8,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{3}=14-8=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(D-9I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$ of dimension $6$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $6,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{2}=6-3=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =9.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559642 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559873 1069 "q-74424" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559873 1071 "Dana surveys $72$ randomly chosen students at her school, asking them if they have gerbils or rabbits at home. Her findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a rabbit | Doesn't have a rabbit | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a gerbil | $18$ | $12$ | $30$ |\n| Doesn't have a gerbil | $6$ | $36$ | $42$ |\n| Totals | $24$ | $48$ | $72$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student at the school has a gerbil and also has a rabbit." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559873 1072 #uuid "e30d37ae-3013-5226-8257-f893cef40757" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559873 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student at the school has a gerbil and also has a rabbit, we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a gerbil\"** and the column **\"Has a rabbit\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a gerbil\"** and the column **\"Has a rabbit\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Has a rabbit | Doesn't have a rabbit | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a gerbil | $18$ | $12$ | $30$ |\n| Doesn't have a gerbil | $6$ | $36$ | $42$ |\n| Totals | $24$ | $48$ | $72$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(72),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{18}{72}=0.25.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559739 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559874 1069 "q-170020" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559874 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5t+2v=13 \\\\ 2t+v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe variable $v$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1\\. Multiply the second equation by $-2$ and obtain\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}-4t-2v=-10.\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}⋯\\,\\,?\\,\\,⋯\\,\\,=3.\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nFind the missing expression, and hence find the solution $(t,v)$ of the system." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559874 1072 #uuid "e48aeb5c-60a2-50ba-8aad-37ecd4e919eb" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559874 1074 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $-2$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5t+2v=13 \\\\ (\\,2t+v=5\\,)\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5t+2v=13 \\\\ -4t-2v=-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $v,$ and solve the resulting equation for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5t+\\cancel{2v} & =13 \\\\ -4t-\\cancel{2v} & =-10 \\\\ (5t-4t) & =(13-10) \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $t=3.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $t=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+v & =5 \\\\ 2(3)+v & =5 \\\\ 6+v & =5 \\\\ v & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $t=3,\\,v=-1,$ which can be expressed as $(3,-1).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559728 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559875 1069 "q-120213" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559875 1071 "A survey asks a group of students about their eye color. The findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Green | Blue | Brown | Black | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $10$ | $12$ | $8$ | $10$ | $40$ |\n| Boys | $13$ | $12$ | $15$ | $20$ | $60$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $24$ | $23$ | $30$ | $100$ |\n\nLet $B$ be the event that a randomly selected student is a **\"Boy\"**, and $C$ be the event that a randomly selected student has **\"Brown\"** eyes. Estimate $P(B\\cap C).$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559875 1072 #uuid "e4bda52d-6631-58b0-bd68-5066bd3ff243" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559875 1074 "First, note that $B\\cap C$ is the event that a randomly selected student is a boy and has brown eyes. \n\nTo estimate the probability of $B\\cap C,$ we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Boys\"** and the column **\"Brown\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Boys\"** and the column **\"Brown\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Green | Blue | Brown | Black | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $10$ | $12$ | $8$ | $10$ | $40$ |\n| Boys | $13$ | $12$ | $15$ | $20$ | $60$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $24$ | $23$ | $30$ | $100$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(B\\cap C)=\\frac{15}{100}=\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559876 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559686 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559876 1069 "q-96757" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559876 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the interval from $0$ to $3$ inclusive.\"*" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559876 1072 #uuid "e8bbc308-2450-589f-b174-679c413f5f6e" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559876 1074 "The sentence *\"The function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the interval from $0$ to $3$ inclusive\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $f:X\\to [0,3]$\n\nHere, the domain of $f$ is $X,$ and the codomain of $f$ is $[0,3].$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559660 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559877 1069 "q-71467" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559877 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559877 1071 "Given the two planes $3x-6y+1=0$ and $-x+2y=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n\nII. The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nIII. The planes intersect in a straight line." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559877 1072 #uuid "e8cdac5c-1e47-510f-8a9c-c4df54b69f10" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559877 1074 "First, notice that:  \n\n- For the plane $3x-6y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-6,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x+2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,-6,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0i+0j+0k \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}=0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are parallel. However,  the second plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the first plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n3(0)-6(0)+1=1\\ne 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559745 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559878 1069 "q-73779" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559878 1071 "For which of the following polynomials is $(x-2)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{3}-4x$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}+4x+5$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{2}-4x+4$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559878 1072 #uuid "e986899c-1ec0-569d-94ba-d6811b53879c" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559878 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=2$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=2$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=2$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & ={2}^{3}-4(2) \\\\  & =8-8 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=2$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={2}^{2}+4(2)+5 \\\\  & =4+8+5 \\\\  & =17\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=2$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(2) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+4 \\\\  & =4-8+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only .\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559675 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559879 1069 "q-73944" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559879 1071 "Let\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ p \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ p\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $v\\cdot w=3$, find the value of $p.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559879 1072 #uuid "ea418b13-9cd8-58b9-8c0f-d233456f224d" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559879 1074 "Starting from the equation $v\\cdot w=3,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $p$ and then solve for $p$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot w & =3 \\\\ -2\\cdot 2+p\\cdot (-4)+5\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot p & =3 \\\\ -4-4p+15+6-3p & =3 \\\\ -7p+17 & =3 \\\\ 7p & =14 \\\\ p & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $p=2.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559670 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559880 1069 "q-95010" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559880 1071 "$$\n{A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Find the matrix $C$ given that $B=\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\},$ and the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $B$ are $[C]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559880 1072 #uuid "ee947d80-6aa8-5dc4-9785-d937ced374de" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559880 1074 "Any vector in $\\text{Span}\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{A}_{1}+{x}_{2}{A}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n[C]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559716 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559881 1069 "q-105993" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559881 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y={x}^{2}+2?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559881 1072 #uuid "efac71ed-19b4-5792-ad4d-1b9a5a0c61f5" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559881 1074 "In general, the graph of $y={x}^{2}+k$ looks like the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ but translated $k$ units up. The vertex of $y={x}^{2}+k$ is located at $(0,k).$\n\nIn the given function $y={x}^{2}+2,$ we have $k=2.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ after being translated $2$ units *up*. The vertex is located at $(0,2).$\n\n![](images/c6/c6e9f0867c9b5c04ed1390b01ece62d1fb33673e1e17373a92b2b2d576aeae05.png)" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559735 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559882 1069 "q-102818" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559882 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x-1}=\\frac{2}{x+3}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559882 1072 #uuid "f03fefa6-3a2e-5378-840e-6b0b6beb7bc1" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559882 1074 "First, note that $x=-3$ and $x=1$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1} & =\\frac{2}{x+3} \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+3) & =(x-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x+3 & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =2x-2 \\\\ x+3-x & =2x-2-x \\\\ 3 & =x-2 \\\\ 3+2 & =x-2+2 \\\\ 5 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=5$ is different from $x=-3$ and $x=1,$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=5.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559690 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559883 1069 "q-96746" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559883 1071 "Which of the following functions has the domain $(-2,0)?$\n\nI. $p:(-2,0)\\to \\{-2,0\\}$\n\nII. $q:(-2,0)\\to (-2,0)$\n\nIII. $r:[-2,0)\\to (0,2)$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559883 1072 #uuid "f312482e-bbe1-5f40-a816-ce63ddb8cbac" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559883 1074 "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is $(-2,0),$ the interval from $-2$ to $0$ (not including $-2$ nor $0$).\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $(-2,0),$ the interval from $-2$ to $0$ (not including $-2$ nor $0$).\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $[-2,0),$ the interval from $-2$ to $0$ (including $-2$ but not including $0$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559755 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559884 1069 "q-175340" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559884 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n\nI. $P(n):$ ${n}^{2}$ is odd.\n\nII. $Q(x):$ $(x+2)|5$\n\nIII. $R(y):$ $y$ is a multiple of $4.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559884 1072 #uuid "f4fa3c45-5e91-57f0-a165-1adda22fdfd5" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559884 1074 "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559731 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559734 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559885 1069 "q-336950" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559885 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}+xy-6 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-{y}^{2}+2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}={[2,\\text{ }1]}^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559885 1072 #uuid "f52e822f-6421-5efd-a62a-efb0d9241bfe" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559885 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy-6 \\\\ -{y}^{2}+2x-3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy-6)=2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy-6)=x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+2x-3)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+2x-3)=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y & x \\\\ 2 & -2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(2)+1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{2}.$\n\nSince the eigenvalues are real and have opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559732 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559886 1069 "q-116315" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559886 1071 "The intensity $I$ of light, measured in $\\text{lux},$ is inversely proportional to the **square** of the distance $d,$ measured in meters, between the light source and the object being illuminated. A device that measures the light intensity is $12$ meters away from the light source and registers $4$ $\\text{lux}.$  What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $d?$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559886 1072 #uuid "f5ab26d2-91d7-599f-b642-d61eec8ddc94" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559886 1074 "Since the intensity $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the distance $d,$ we must have\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $I=4\\,\\text{lux}$ when $d=12\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =\\frac{k}{{12}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{k}{144} \\\\ 4\\cdot 144 & =k \\\\ 576 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{576}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559767 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559887 1069 "q-47794" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559887 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{9}{6x-3}=\\frac{2}{3x+1}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559887 1072 #uuid "f7ee9ca4-a3f0-5a8a-a26b-4c46c8822ae6" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559887 1074 "First, note that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{6x-3} & =\\frac{2}{3x+1} \\\\ 9\\cdot (3x+1) & =(6x-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 27x+9 & =12x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27x+9 & =12x-6 \\\\ 27x+9-12x & =12x-6-12x \\\\ 15x+9 & =-6 \\\\ 15x+9-9 & =-6-9 \\\\ 15x & =-15 \\\\ \\frac{15x}{15} & =\\frac{-15}{15} \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x=-1$ is different from $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can accept it as a solution.\nThus, the solution is $x=-1.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559712 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559888 1069 "q-104937" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559888 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $h$ maps the interval from $0$ to $1$ exclusive to the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $1$ only.\"*" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559888 1072 #uuid "f9c7a6ed-7bc4-5fae-bd01-cf7380224c4e" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559888 1074 "The sentence *\"The function $h$ maps the interval from $0$ to $1$ exclusive to the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $1$ only\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $h:(0,1)\\to \\{0,1\\}$\n\nHere, the domain of $h$ is $(0,1),$ and the codomain of $h$ is $\\{0,1\\}.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559691 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559889 1069 "q-71414" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559889 1071 "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+2z=0$ and $4x-2y+4z+8=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559889 1072 #uuid "fad299b6-2750-5729-9622-52ccf21ff85a" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559889 1074 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\nx+2(0)+2z=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ and $x=-2z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y+4z+8 & =0 \\\\ 4(-2z)-2(0)+4z+8 & =0 \\\\ -4z & =-8 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2z=-4$ and $a=⟨-4,0,2⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $4x-2y+4z+8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨4,-2,4⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,2⟩\\times ⟨4,-2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =12i+4j-10k \\\\  & =⟨12,4,-10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{2}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨6,2,-5⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-4,0,2⟩+t⟨6,2,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-4+2=-2,$ and $b=⟨6,2,-5⟩.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559746 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559890 1069 "q-44817" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559890 1071 "A  recording studio charges musicians an initial fee of $$80$ to record an album. The studio also charges an additional $$50$ per hour.\n\nWhat expression gives the cost, in dollars, of recording an album,  in terms of the number of hours $h$ used?" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559890 1072 #uuid "fdafad3a-7759-57a9-8969-df2e3e1dfbfc" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559890 1074 "The fixed fee for recording an album is $$80.$ Hence,  $80$ should be a constant term.\n\nNow, if $h$ is the number of hours used to record an album, the cost  for $h$ hours of a recording must be $50h$ dollars.\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost, in dollars, of recording an album will be $50h+80.$" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559647 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559891 1069 "q-95279" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559891 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ has two negative singular values.\n\nII. Any $4\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. Any $8\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values." 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559891 1072 #uuid "ffe00adb-b1a9-53a2-98b2-87735ab0e412" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559891 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.  All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, \n$A$  can't have any negative singular value.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $4\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $8\\times 4$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $4\\times 8$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $4\\times 4$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{4\\times 8}\\times {A}_{8\\times 4}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{4\\times 4}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559892 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559976 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559892 1062 #uuid "00649106-db72-5f98-a26b-cf0c8648ce5c" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559892 1063 #edb/ref 17592186366636 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559893 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559980 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559893 1062 #uuid "00b2cd0a-8e39-5570-a66f-846996a620a2" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559893 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559792 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559894 1062 #uuid "00ddeb7a-74b2-5bc8-804f-2e9a90acf07e" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559894 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559890 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559895 1062 #uuid "04d8a637-3615-5a68-9997-f32c9376b669" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559895 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559801 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559896 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559912 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559896 1062 #uuid "05656f4c-c5e5-5079-9eb0-b865d0bab77a" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559896 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559878 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559897 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559894 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559897 1062 #uuid "063c8e6a-a08e-5ad8-a3f1-802aa4c7ae5e" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559897 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559871 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559898 1062 #uuid "06eb6fa1-dfb2-5b2c-acc7-dce9cf39f5b3" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559898 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559882 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559899 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559979 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559899 1062 #uuid "088839b5-0f82-54e8-b0eb-73896d309a03" 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559899 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559786 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559900 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559932 13194139536032 17592186045417 true] [17592186559900 1062 #uuid "0a2276bc-3326-51a6-9c69-6590d1bce6ac" 13194139536032 17592